// Yoopanese (U.P. dialect) translation of the 323 Water War tiers, keyed by the exact English label.
window.YOOPER = window.YOOPER || {};
window.YOOPER.WATER_WAR_TIERS = Object.assign(window.YOOPER.WATER_WAR_TIERS || {}, {
  "No Water Drawn": { label: "No Water Drawn", desc: "It’s 68 degrees and nobody wants to get wet, ya know. Da garden hose stays coiled in 14 loops on its reel, da balloons stay sealed in dere bag of 100, and da only water in play is a glass on da porch rail nobody’s touched in 40 minutes. Zero throws tried, zero landed. Da summer opens wit’ da ledger blank." },
  "Sketch of a Pump": { label: "Sketch of a Pump", desc: "Nobody’s fightin’, so da war gets fought on paper instead. A pump gets drawn at 4 squares to da inch: a tube, a plunger, a handle, and an arrow labeled “water comes out here.” Nothin’ on it’s got a dimension, dough da tube happens to be exactly 4 squares wide, and da handle’s drawn 3 times longer dan da tube it works. It’s da first page of a design record dat’ll outlast da weather." },
  "Humidity Only": { label: "Humidity Only", desc: "Water’s in da yard as vapor and noplace else, eh. At 68 degrees and 40 percent humidity, da first 10 feet of air over a 40-by-60-foot lawn holds about 10 pounds of it, and none of it can be cupped, aimed, or t’rown. No gauge on da block is pointed at it, and no way of arrangin’ it makes a projectile." },
  "Pump Specs Drawn": { label: "Pump Specs Drawn", desc: "Da sketch picks up numbers: a 1-inch bore, a 6-inch stroke, and 4.7 cubic inches a pull, about 2.6 ounces. Dat’s 2 strokes to fill a 4-ounce paper cup and roughly 4,900 to empty a 100-gallon kiddie pool, which is still folded up in da garage. Water in play stands at 0 ounces. Da drawin’ gets checked twice and comes out consistent wit’ itself, which is more dan ya can say for da weather." },
  "Breath on Glass": { label: "Breat’ on Glass", desc: "Water’s technically dere as fog breathed onto a glass fresh outta da fridge, about 1 micron thick and gone in 4 seconds. Ya need a flashlight held at an angle to even see it, and wipin’ da whole supply into yer palm don’t change da mass, shape, or temperature one bit. Block-wide yield works out to a thousandth of 1 squirt, eh." },
  "Stroke Length Argument": { label: "Stroke Lengt’ Argument", desc: "Two folks spend a whole afternoon on whedder da stroke oughta be 6 inches or 8. Six wins on account of an 8-inch pull, at about 3.5 ounces, needs a longer arm dan da 11-year-old who’s gonna be workin’ it, and nobody can grow fast enough to prove udderwise. Da argument gets settled wit’out any water at all: it’s 66 degrees, da nearest supply is a glass of iced tea on da porch rail, and neider one goes outside to check." },
  "Hygrometer Reading": { label: "Hygrometer Readin’", desc: "Humidity gets up to 44 percent, enough for a hygrometer to read and not enough fer a hand to feel. Two separate hygrometers, 1 in da kitchen and 1 on da porch, agree inside 10 percent, which is da best evidence of water anybody’s gonna collect dis week. Nothin’ can be filled, squeezed, or t’rown." },
  "Paper-Tube Mock-Up": { label: "Paper-Tube Mock-Up", desc: "A full-size shell gets rolled from a paper-towel tube, taped down to a 1-inch bore, and fitted wit’ a pencil fer a plunger to prove it fits a 7-year-old’s grip. It does, at 14 inches overall, wit’ da handle clearin’ da elbow by 2 inches at full stroke, which was da whole point. It can’t move any water, holds no seal, and goes soggy when it’s set down on a damp patch of lawn, of which dere’s 1, under da downspout." },
  "Theoretical Splash": { label: "Theoretical Splash", desc: "Da water balloon exists as a math object wit’ a set volume, burst pressure, and trajectory. Ya can describe it, diagram it, and spec it out to 4 decimal places, but at 67 degrees nobody’s gonna open da bag of 100 to put one togedder. Every planned fight comes out to 0 t’rows a side." },
  "Valves Laid Out": { label: "Valves Laid Out", desc: "Da plumbin’ gets worked out end to end: a foot valve at da intake, a check valve at da nozzle, a cup seal on da plunger, and a spring relief valve set to open at 15 psi so a plugged nozzle bleeds out a side port instead of splittin’ da tube or kickin’ da handle into somebody’s chin. Every number in it is borrowed from a catalog and none of ’em’s been tested. Water on hand stands at 0 ounces, so da relief valve’s got nobody to protect yet." },
  "Anecdotal Soaking": { label: "Anecdotal Soakin’", desc: "Somebody tells about soakin’ a cousin last July. Da story’s real detailed — a full 2-gallon bucket, t’rown from 6 feet, a direct hit — and none of it can be checked. Four witnesses were s’posedly dere and 2 of ’em won’t back it up. Today’s high is 70 degrees." },
  "First Plunger Cut": { label: "First Plunger Cut", desc: "Nine hours wit’ a borrowed drill and 3 grades of sandpaper whittle a broom handle down to a plunger rod, spun in da chuck against da paper ’cause nuttin’ else in da house turns. It comes out 0.96 inches at one end and 0.99 at da udder, and oval by about a sixtieth of an inch. It slides in da hand. Dere’s nuttin’ to push wit’ it: da only water in reach is da dog’s bowl, and da dog’s usin’ it." },
  "Unconfirmed Splash": { label: "Unconfirmed Splash", desc: "Somebody claims dey landed a flicked drop on a sibling from 5 feet. It don’t happen again in an estimated 40 more flicks, dere’s no picture, and da wet spot, if dere ever was one, dries up in about 90 seconds at 71 degrees. Da claim gets logged, argued over, and left hangin’ fer da season." },
  "Bore Fit Check": { label: "Bore Fit Check", desc: "Da plunger gets pushed into a 14-inch piece of 1-inch PVC and da gap’s measured at 8 spots around da cup seal. Da target was snug; da readings go from a press fit to a gap ya could slide a playin’ card t’rough, da oval catchin’ at 2 o’clock every stroke. It gets sanded back by hand over about 40 minutes. Still no water, and da check goes down as passed." },
  "Dewdrop Dare": { label: "Dewdrop Dare", desc: "At 7:15 in da mornin’ da lawn’s got dew on it, and somebody gets dared to cross it barefoot. Da crossin’ is 22 feet, takes 9 seconds, and leaves both feet wet to da ankle. One side calls it an act of war and da udder calls it weather, and da block splits about even. Neider side comes up wit’ a sample, and da dew’s gone by 9:40." },
  "Relief Valve Bench Test": { label: "Relief Valve Bench Test", desc: "Da relief valve gets loaded up wit’ a bicycle pump and a tire gauge till it pops, 31 times in a row, at a pressure dat varies under 8 percent from one try to da next. It’s da first number in da whole project dat got measured instead of picked. Water in play: a damp dish towel on da oven handle, holdin’ maybe 2 ounces, none of it able to plug anyt’ing." },
  "Condensation Ring": { label: "Condensation Ring", desc: "Da first loose moisture of da war is a ring on a patio table, 3 inches across, left by a glass of iced tea. Somebody drags a finger t’rough it and flicks roughly 0.05 mL at a sibling from 2 feet away. Da sibling reports no contact. Total block-wide yield is 1 iffy flick, and it don’t land." },
  "Dry Stroke, No Prime": { label: "Dry Stroke, No Prime", desc: "Da finished pump gets worked on da kitchen floor wit’ an empty intake to check dat da handle pulls da plunger its full 6 inches and dat both valves click shut da second da stroke turns around. Both hold, over 11 strokes. Da only output is a wheeze from da intake and a little click from da outlet, and da operator gets told twice to go do dat outside." },
  "Wet Footprints": { label: "Wet Footprints", desc: "Barefoot prints from da bathtub to da back door show up at a glance from across da porch, by da shine, and disappear at arm’s length. Dey hold dere outline fer about a minute at 72 degrees before dryin’ back to bare boards. Moppin’ all 400 square feet of porch wit’ a sponge gets ya about a teaspoon, which lasts roughly 1 second in a closed hand." },
  "Bathtub Trial": { label: "Bathtub Trial", desc: "Testin’ starts indoors wit’ da only standin’ water in da house. Da intake goes into a bathtub run 4 inches deep, and 40 strokes move about 104 ounces across da bathroom at 2.6 ounces a stroke, inside 6 percent of da drawin’ by measurin’ jug. Da pump is proven. Da bathroom, da hallway, and 1 linen closet don’t recover fer 2 days, and da trial don’t get repeated." },
  "Flicked Fingertip": { label: "Flicked Fingertip", desc: "Wipin’ da outside of a sweatin’ lemonade glass fer 6 minutes gets ya enough to wet 1 fingertip, about 3 drops. Flicked at da back of a neck, it breaks up inside 4 feet and leaves no mark anybody can find. Two players, 1 flick apiece, and da glass is dry inside a minute." },
  "Measuring Cup Calibration": { label: "Measurin’ Cup Calibration", desc: "Measurin’ cups take over from da bathtub on account of ya can pour ’em back. Runs at 3 handle speeds — slow, walkin’ pace, and frantic — give 2.6, 2.5, and 1.9 ounces a stroke, da fast ones leakin’ past da cup seal on da way back. Da pump is confirmed to meter instead of just splash. Outdoor temperature dat week tops out at 77 degrees and stays out of it." },
  "Bottle Cap Toss": { label: "Bottle Cap Toss", desc: "A bottle cap holds about 7 mL, which is a thimble’s worth wit’ a bit left over. Tossed underhand it hangs togedder as one blob fer about 6 feet. 3 or 4 capfuls can be in play at once if every water bottle in da house gets drained fer ’em, 9 bottles over 20 minutes." },
  "Pond Water Run": { label: "Pond Water Run", desc: "A bucket of water from da retention pond proves da pump against somethin’ wit’ real grit: 11 gallons t’rough da barrel in one afternoon wit’out a clog, dough da foot valve loses prime about 1 stroke in 6 whenever da intake lifts off da bottom. Lost prime gets wrote down as da design’s weakest spot and don’t get fixed. Everybody agrees nobody’s ever gettin’ squirted wit’ pond water, and nobody here’s squirted anybody wit’ anyt’ing yet." },
  "Windshield Dew": { label: "Windshield Dew", desc: "Wipe da whole windshield of a car at 6:50 in da mornin’, roughly 15 square feet, and ya get one cupped palmful wit’ no real volume to it. It lasts about 2 feet of flight before breakin’ into spray. A determined pair workin’ da whole block, 31 windshields over 25 minutes, can make 5 or 6 t’rows, and none of ’em get dere as anyt’ing." },
  "Pavement Dots": { label: "Pavement Dots", desc: "Somebody shows up wit’ da pump and no water fight to use it in, so da intake goes into da leftover calibration water, da same 4 cups da pump got measured on back in May. Da handle gets run to full stroke and 11 shots get laid down in a perfect dotted line across 4 feet of hot driveway, every dot gone inside 2 minutes at 79 degrees. Everybody dere agrees dis ain’t an attack. It goes in da record anyhow, ’cause da pump worked perfect and somebody oughta say so." },
  "Stroke Stops Set": { label: "Stroke Stops Set", desc: "After da dotted line da handle gets 4 hard stops instead of a free slide: locked, 1.5 inches fer a sprinkle, 3 fer a squirt, and da full 6 fer 2.6 ounces. Every stop gets checked by firin’ 10 strokes into a measurin’ jug and weighin’ what lands. Repeatability across 10 runs holds inside 9 percent, which is better dan any summer da pump was built fer has ever managed, eh." },
  "Birdbath Skimming": { label: "Birdbat’ Skimmin’", desc: "Da birdbath, 1 inch deep and 14 inches across, gets skimmed by cupped hand. Yield runs about 1 handful a dip, mostly seed husks and 1 feather by volume. 10 to 12 can be scooped in 20 minutes, and roughly 2 in 3 drain out t’rough da fingers before ya let go." },
  "Lost Prime Failure": { label: "Lost Prime Failure", desc: "Da first honest failure. Drawin’ from a puddle barely 1 inch deep, da intake sucks air and da plunger strokes wit’out movin’ any water, output droppin’ to a third of da bench number over da same 20 strokes. Nuttin’ breaks, which is da design doin’ what it’s s’posed to: a pump dat loses prime goes quiet instead of splittin’ its barrel. Da result gets wrote up in full, includin’ da part where it didn’t work." },
  "Puddle Stomp": { label: "Puddle Stomp", desc: "A leftover sprinkler puddle, 18 inches across and half an inch deep, is plainly water and plainly not enough of it. One stomp t’rows spray about 3 feet every which way and soaks 2 pairs of socks, neider one belongin’ to da stomper. Da puddle’s good fer about 2 stomps apiece fer 4 people, and nobody t’inks to refill it." },
  "Weighted Strainer Retrofit": { label: "Weighted Strainer Retrofit", desc: "A brass fishin’ sinker and a strainer drilled wit’ 8 holes get fitted onto da intake, on da idea dat a hose end dat can’t float can’t lose prime. Draw comes back to about 80 percent of bench output from da same 1-inch puddle. Da strainer now slurps every stroke wit’ a noise 4 different people take fer a small animal." },
  "Half a Glass": { label: "Half a Glass", desc: "Just shy of a real t’row: a glass holdin’ about 4 ounces, which has to stay in yer hand ’cause da glass ain’t allowed outside. Flung, roughly half gets to da target and da rest lands on da t’rower’s own shoes. Da yard’s whole supply is good fer 1 usable t’row and 1 dat’s gonna get argued about." },
  "First Live Target": { label: "First Live Target", desc: "Da pump meets a person fer da first time, wit’ 19 ounces bailed out by hand from da bottom of a drained kiddie pool. It fires 7 full strokes before suckin’ air, reachin’ 14 feet and landin’ 3. Da wet patch is plain as day, runs down one side of a T-shirt, and dries in 4 minutes at 81 degrees. Pictures get taken up close so da dry rest of da shirt don’t show." },
  "One Bad Balloon": { label: "One Bad Balloon", desc: "One balloon, da last one in a party bag from March, gets filled to maybe 2 ounces and tied wit’ a knot dat’s mostly hope. It gets t’rown once from about 20 feet, misses, bounces twice on da grass, and don’t break. No balloons left fer a second try, and da whole fight lasts under 10 seconds wit’ 1 player a side." },
  "Clogged Pump": { label: "Clogged Pump", desc: "Da block’s one pump sucks a grass clippin’ into da outlet check valve, and da relief valve does exactly what it was built fer: da handle keeps strokin’, nuttin’ splits, and water dribbles out da side port instead of da nozzle. Clearin’ it takes 40 seconds wit’ da nozzle unscrewed. Durin’ dose 40 seconds da operator’s defenseless, gets hit twice, and files da summer’s first formal complaint about equipment reliability." },
  "Relief Valve Opens As Designed": { label: "Relief Valve Opens Like It Oughta", desc: "A second clog, a maple seed dis time, and da relief valve opens before da operator notices anyt’ing but a change in da sound. Total holdup is 9 seconds. Da bench number from da bicycle pump holds in 84-degree heat to inside 11 percent, which retires da last untested number in da pump. It’s da only piece of gear on da block dat’s never broke." },
  "One Good Balloon": { label: "One Good Balloon", desc: "Enough patience fer one balloon done right: 5 ounces, tied tight, no air pocket. It carries 35 feet and bursts on contact just like it oughta. Dere’s exactly 1 on da property, both sides know where it’s at, it changes hands twice before anybody t’rows it, and da whole fight is a single t’row." },
  "Full-Stroke Spec Met": { label: "Full-Stroke Spec Met", desc: "Da spec gets met outdoors fer da first time: 2.6 ounces a stroke at 1 stroke a second, measured into a jug over 20 strokes wit’ a variation of 7 percent, at a range of 30 feet. From da porch da target looks soaked t’rough. Up close it’s one stripe down da left side, and da operator spends da next 20 minutes walkin’ da target around in a slow circle to fix dat." },
  "Two Balloons": { label: "Two Balloons", desc: "Enough fer exactly 1 t’row each, 2 balloons at about 5 ounces apiece. Both get let go inside da same second from 30 feet, 1 connects, and da yard, about 40 feet by 60, is outta ammo after. Da fight runs 4 seconds and total supply at da openin’ t’row is 2." },
  "Intake Hose Deployed": { label: "Intake Hose Deployed", desc: "Da 10-foot intake hose gets used fer real da first time, its weighted strainer dropped into a kiddie pool holdin’ 100 gallons while da operator stands at da rim. Run length quits bein’ limited by da 1-quart canteen clipped under da barrel and starts bein’ limited by da pool, which is da whole point of da design. One session fires 60 strokes wit’out a refill." },
  "Handful of Balloons": { label: "Handful of Balloons", desc: "A stockpile of 6 to 8 balloons fits in one mixin’ bowl. Ammo gets rationed right off at 2 t’rows a minute and it’s all gone inside 90 seconds of da openin’ exchange. 2 to 4 players, no set positions, and no refills, since da only one who can tie a knot went inside." },
  "Air Chamber Proof": { label: "Air Chamber Proof", desc: "Da pump gets fed in stop-and-go bursts on purpose to see if da air pocket trapped at da top of da 1-quart canteen smooths out da stream or just holds water. Variation in stream reach runs 14 percent wit’ da canteen in line against 29 percent wit’out it, over 3 matched runs at 90 degrees. One part’s doin’ 2 jobs, pulse damper on da intake hose and reservoir off of it, and it’s da last piece of da design nobody’d proven." },
  "Dish Towel Sling": { label: "Dish Towel Sling", desc: "Da first try at a launcher: a balloon set in a dish towel, swung twice overhead by 2 corners, and let go on feel alone. It goes 45 feet against a hand t’row’s 25, and about 1 in 9 lands inside 10 feet of whatever it was aimed at. Two towels and 3 balloons end up on da roof in da first hour." },
  "Rotary Crank Prototype": { label: "Rotary Crank Prototype", desc: "An older sibling drags out da rejected rotary design, a hand-cranked gear pump dat wants da operator to turn a handle and aim a nozzle at da same time. It t’rows a decent 20-foot stream fer about 30 seconds, and den da operator’s arm gives out. Two of ’em exist on da block and neider one makes it t’rough da afternoon in one piece. Da plunger pump is agreed, once again and fer da record, to’ve been da right design." },
  "The Rotary Is Abandoned": { label: "Da Rotary Gets Ditched", desc: "Both rotary pumps get stripped fer parts inside 1 hour of failin’, and da decision goes in da record wit’ a reason instead of a verdict: a machine dat wants a crankin’ arm and an aimin’ arm at once is 1 arm short. Da plunger design stands unchallenged from here on out. Da crank handles get kept and used, over da next 3 weeks, fer totally unrelated stuff." },
  "Cargo Pocket Load": { label: "Cargo Pocket Load", desc: "10 to 12 balloons get carried in cargo shorts ’cause dere’s noplace else to put ’em, and 3 bust in da pockets before da first t’row. Effective range gets to 25 feet and da hit rate runs about 1 in 5. Both sides open at full supply and da fight’s decided inside 2 minutes by whoever t’rows slower." },
  "Continuous Intake Draw": { label: "Continuous Intake Draw", desc: "Da main mode goes live: stand at da pool rim, strainer on da bottom, and fire forever wit’out ever unscrewin’ da canteen. 240 strokes, about 4.9 gallons, go out in one session of 6 minutes straight. Ammo supply quits dependin’ on who’s got da spigot and starts dependin’ on who’s willin’ to pump." },
  "Balloon Skirmish": { label: "Balloon Skirmish", desc: "Real water balloons, fer a minute dere: roughly a dozen a side at about 5 ounces each. Both sides run dry inside 2 minutes, before anybody gets to cover, and da fight ends on empty supply instead of a decision. Hit rate holds around 1 in 4 at da usual 25-foot range, at 91 degrees in da shade." },
  "Ladle Lob": { label: "Ladle Lob", desc: "A plastic soup ladle turns out to cradle a balloon and fling it wit’out squeezin’ it. Range runs 50 feet, da balloon makes it t’rough release in one piece 9 times outta 10, and 1 person can load and t’row wit’out settin’ nuttin’ down. It becomes da go-to fer anybody who can’t t’row hard, and da kitchen’s short 1 ladle da rest of da summer." },
  "Second Pump Built": { label: "Second Pump Built", desc: "A second pump gets built from da same drawin’ over 2 evenings, wit’ da oval problem fixed by buyin’ a factory dowel fer $1.29 instead of sandin’ down a broom handle. Da two of ’em come out inside 4 percent of each udder on ounces a stroke, which makes da design repeatable instead of lucky. From here on dere’s a fleet, of 2." },
  "Around the Garage": { label: "Around Da Garage", desc: "Supply gets up to about 18 balloons a side, enough dat 1 player can peel off and circle around wit’out leavin’ da line empty. Da first sneaky approach of da summer covers 60 feet in 11 seconds around da back of da garage, da runner carryin’ 5 balloons and gettin’ to 12 feet before anybody looks. It works once, and both sides put a lookout on da garage side from den on." },
  "Pantyhose Sling": { label: "Pantyhose Sling", desc: "A balloon goes in da toe of an old pantyhose leg, gets swung underhand, and gets let go at da bottom of da arc. It gets 60 feet at full stretch, dough effective range stays near 35 ’cause 1 t’row in 8 lands anywhere close and 1 in 5 busts mid-swing. Four people get soaked by dere own release da first afternoon, and it stays in service anyhow." },
  "Hedge Ambush": { label: "Hedge Ambush", desc: "Enough to crouch behind da hedge wit’ 3 balloons in reserve and 15 total. Position starts to matter, and holdin’ fire turns into a real option, dough 2 tries in 3 never go off ’cause somebody giggles first. Fights run 3 to 5 minutes, open after an average 4-minute wait from under 15 feet wit’ a hit rate of 1 in 2, and come down to who’s holdin’ more when da first volley’s done." },
  "Pump Lent Out": { label: "Pump Lent Out", desc: "A pump leaves da property fer da first time, lent to a house 4 doors down on da condition it comes back da same day. It does, 3 hours late, wit’ grass in da strainer and da cup seal rolled half outta its groove. Four more households ask fer one inside da week and get told dere’s 2 in existence." },
  "Squirt Gun Standoff": { label: "Squirt Gun Standoff", desc: "Both sides got a full squirt gun, about 30 pulls each, and neider one’s gonna spend da first one, eh. Da pause runs 4 to 6 minutes at 16 feet, just past da guns’ 15-foot reach, wit’ roughly 1 test shot a minute dat lands on da grass in between. Both sides are sweatin’ plain as day at 81°F. It gets settled when a t’ird kid shows up wit’ a garden hose and joins whichever side’s closer to da spigot." },
  "Bucket Sling": { label: "Bucket Sling", desc: "A 2-gallon paint bucket gets swung underhand to t’row a whole sheet of water instead of a stream at somebody. Nuttin’ goes past 12 feet and nuttin’ gets aimed, but da sheet spreads across a front about 10 feet wide, da first time anybody goes after an area instead of a sibling. Refillin’ at da spigot takes 25 seconds against a squirt gun’s 6, and da t’rower’s own shins catch about a quarter of every load, ya know." },
  "Tandem Pumps": { label: "Tandem Pumps", desc: "Both pumps feed da same hose t’rough a Y-fittin’, da second guy strokin’ on da first guy’s return so da stream never quits. One pump by itself spurts 2.6 ounces and stops; 2 takin’ turns give ya an unbroken stream of roughly 1.2 gallons a minute. Callin’ out da rhythm falls apart inside 40 seconds, so da second guy just watches da first guy’s handle instead, eh." },
  "Pistol Duel": { label: "Pistol Duel", desc: "Two kids who mean it, 1 squirt pistol and about 30 pulls apiece, and a crowd of 4 to 6 on da porch steps. Da rules get set ahead of time: back to back, 10 paces, shoulders to knees only, and 1 refill each at da spigot. First to 5 confirmed hits takes it, a confirmed hit bein’ a dark spot da size of a quarter, and da whole t’ing runs 8 to 12 minutes in 84°F heat." },
  "Transfer Contest": { label: "Transfer Contest", desc: "Da first contest wit’ da t’ing: 2 guys, 2 pumps, 20 minutes, most water moved from a kiddie pool into empty buckets. Da winner moves 11.5 gallons, about 570 strokes, and gets disqualified when it turns out da buckets drained right back into da pool t’rough da intake hose, which nobody ever lifted out. Da rule dat water only counts if somebody could t’row it goes in da record and never comes out, eh." },
  "Everybody Against Everybody": { label: "Everybody Against Everybody", desc: "Eight or nine kids, no teams, and a refill rule of 1 trip to da hose a minute. Alliances last under 30 seconds and average 4 per kid before it’s over, usually busted up by a point-blank squirt from da ally. It runs 15 minutes at 86°F, nobody agrees who won, and it ends when da hose nozzle turns up under da hydrangea wit’ nobody ownin’ up to puttin’ it dere." },
  "Pump Rivalry": { label: "Pump Rivalry", desc: "Both sides of da fight get demselves a pump, and da war gets a second front, fought over da kiddie pool instead of wit’ it. Neider pump guy fires one shot all afternoon. Each one moves about 9 gallons, some 440 strokes, from da pool to dere own side’s buckets, which is more water dan dere whole side t’rew. Both come home soaked to da elbow and get told dey missed da real battle, holy wah." },
  "Water Fight": { label: "Water Fight", desc: "A real water fight finally: 6 to 10 kids, cover behind parked cars, and a runnin’ supply of balloons refilled in da middle of it at about 40 a side. Good range goes out to 35 feet wit’ a pistol and 25 wit’ a t’row. How long it lasts depends on when somebody’s ma leans out da screen door, which at 87°F means 20 to 30 minutes, ya know." },
  "Pump Patent Dispute": { label: "Pump Patent Dispute", desc: "Two households both say da pump’s dere own design: 1-inch bore, 6-inch stroke, 2.6 ounces a pull, relief valve set to da number from da bench test. Da case gets heard on a porch by a panel of 3 older kids. Da original goes in as evidence: graph paper at 4 squares to da inch, an arrow labeled “water comes out here,” dated in crayon in da corner. Da panel rules for da kid who drew it and lets da udder household build from da drawin’, so long as every barrel’s got a credit line on it in marker." },
  "Counterfeit Pumps": { label: "Counterfeit Pumps", desc: "Pumps start showin’ up dat weren’t built from da drawin’: wrong bore, a plunger cut out of a bottle cap instead of a washer, no relief valve at all. T’ree of da 4 quit inside a week, 2 by splittin’ da barrel at da elbow under pressure, exactly da way da relief valve’s dere to stop. Da real design gets a marker stripe around da barrel to tell it apart, and da stripe gets copied inside 9 days, eh." },
  "Spigot-to-Spigot": { label: "Spigot-to-Spigot", desc: "Da fight quits holdin’ still and moves across 3 yards and a driveway, coverin’ 400 feet in half an hour at 91°F. Guns get refilled on da run from every outdoor spigot ya pass, about 1 refill every 2 minutes, mostly witout askin’. Neider side holds a spot more dan 90 seconds, and nobody’s ever more dan half full." },
  "Workbench Production": { label: "Workbench Production", desc: "Production moves to a workbench in da garage: PVC cut to da drawin’, glue left 2 hours to set, about 6 hours a pump and 4 pumps a week if nuttin’ leaks. Da holdup quits bein’ da design and turns into 1-inch elbows, and da hardware store only keeps 6 of dose. Da kid at da workbench, who never fired one shot in da whole war, ends up da most important person on da block, holy wah." },
  "Sapling Launcher": { label: "Sapling Launcher", desc: "Surgical tubin’ gets stretched between 2 saplings, and a second guy lyin’ flat on da lawn pulls da balloon pouch back. Max range gets out to 80 feet, twice anyt’ing t’rown by hand, and once it’s tied ya can’t move it. About 1 balloon in 3 busts right in da pouch and soaks da guy lyin’ flat. It comes down after 4 days when da near sapling pulls right out of da ground." },
  "Pruned Fingers": { label: "Pruned Fingers", desc: "Now da ammo outlasts da kids, wit’ each side holdin’ 80 or more filled balloons at any time. Fingertips go all pruny around da 40-minute mark, da fightin’ stops 15 to 20 minutes while everybody goes inside for sunscreen and a glass of water, and den it starts right back up full bore. Da balloons left out in da sun warm up from 70°F to 90°F meanwhile, which nobody counts as a loss." },
  "Stroke Doctrine": { label: "Stroke Doctrine", desc: "A written rule shows up: full 6-inch strokes only, no pumpin’ da barrel dry, which cooks da washer, and no intake from anyt’ing somebody drinks out of. Da last of da 3 is da only one anybody enforces, and dey enforce it all da way. Two pump guys get benched for a week over one draw of 4 strokes from da dog’s bowl, and neider one argues, eh." },
  "Sprinkler Fort": { label: "Sprinkler Fort", desc: "Da first fort of any kind don’t get built so much as switched on: a spinnin’ sprinkler in da middle of da yard wets a circle 20 feet across, and dat circle gets declared held ground. Da defenders crouch behind an upside-down laundry basket 16 inches high in da middle, 4 at a time if dey squeeze. Dere’s no walls, just a 12-second spin everybody learns to time. Da fort era starts wit’ a $6 sprinkler, ya know." },
  "Attrition at the Spigot": { label: "Attrition at Da Spigot", desc: "Neider side can be run off and neider one can refill faster dan it fires. Balloons get filled and tied at 4 a minute a side against 6 gettin’ t’rown. Fights end now wit’ empty buckets after 70 to 90 minutes, wit’ total hits in da low hundreds and da grass underfoot gone to mud at 93°F." },
  "Escorted Pumps": { label: "Escorted Pumps", desc: "A pump out in da open is a sittin’ duck wit’ a kid bent over it at 30 strokes a minute, and both sides figure dat out da same afternoon. Da pump guys each get 2 shooters whose only job is keepin’ da water movin’. Gallons moved an hour drop by a t’ird, and finished refills go from under half to 9 in 10." },
  "Pitched Water Battle": { label: "Pitched Water Battle", desc: "Dere’s enough water now for steady volleys from fixed spots at 6 to 8 t’rows a minute a side, half of ’em flung from a beach towel held by 2 kids at 55 feet against a hand t’row’s 35. Somebody keeps score, hits get called out loud, and da runnin’ count gets argued about every t’ird call or so. Fights go past an hour and take in 8 to 14 kids." },
  "Tarp Rewetting": { label: "Tarp Rewettin’", desc: "Da pumps get turned on da forts demselves, layin’ a fresh film of water over tarps and lawn chairs baked dull by 3 days of 95°F sun. It don’t add nuttin’ structural. It makes da works shine from 40 feet like somebody’s still holdin’ ’em, which is worth more dan da 2 quarts it costs, and da habit sticks around for every later stretch of da war." },
  "Hedge Trench": { label: "Hedge Trench", desc: "Dese spots get scraped out instead of built up: a shallow trench 8 inches deep, 20 inches wide, and 20 feet long along da hedge, wit’ da dirt banked in front to 10 inches high. It stops a low squirt and nuttin’ else, ya can’t swing a towel launcher from inside it, and hose runoff leaves da guys in it layin’ in 2 inches of mud. Two of dese lines face each udder at 50 feet and neider one gets given up da rest of da afternoon." },
  "Pump Sabotage": { label: "Pump Sabotage", desc: "Somebody dumps sand in an intake bucket nobody’s watchin’ overnight. Da sock rubber-banded over da intake for a strainer does its job on da first stroke and no part of da pump gets hurt, but da sock’s gotta be rinsed by hand in da dark, 3 times, and da mornin’ refill of 40 balloons don’t happen. It’s da first attack in da war aimed at gear instead of people, and it works better dan eider, eh." },
  "Tarp Wall": { label: "Tarp Wall", desc: "Da first t’ing built all season: a blue tarp clipped to da clothesline wit’ 5 binder clips and weighed down along da bottom wit’ 2 bricks, hung up in about 15 minutes. It runs 10 feet long and 5 feet high, enough to stop a straight squirt from 15 feet out, but not a balloon lobbed over da top. One wall, one side, and nuttin’ facin’ it yet." },
  "Backpack Pump": { label: "Backpack Pump", desc: "Standard issue shows up for everybody: a 3-pound pump dat rides in a school backpack wit’ 6 feet of intake hose coiled next to it and fills a 3-liter bladder in about 40 strokes. It don’t make water; it moves it, suckin’ up da puddles a fight leaves on da patio and driveway into a reserve behind da line. One guy gets back 2 gallons of spent ammo in da time a filler ties 20 balloons. Every fort crew puts in for one, ya know." },
  "Two-Inch Pump": { label: "Two-Inch Pump", desc: "A bigger pump shows up wit’ a 2-inch bore and da same 6-inch stroke, movin’ about 10.4 ounces a pull, 4 times da original, off a frame bolted to a garden cart. It won’t fit in any backpack and it takes 2 guys to lift it into a wagon. Da trade gets made right out in da open: carryin’ it traded for volume, on a design whose whole point was dat a 9-year-old could haul it around, eh." },
  "Racquet Serve": { label: "Racquet Serve", desc: "Balloons start gettin’ hit instead of t’rown, wit’ a plastic bat and a warped tennis racquet swiped from da garage, neider one given back before fall. A clean hit carries 65 feet, near double a hand t’row, but on da racquet 3 balloons in 5 bust on da strings and spray da hitter instead. Balloons get filled smaller, to about 3 ounces, so da rubber stays t’ick enough to take da hit." },
  "Elbow Tarp": { label: "Elbow Tarp", desc: "Da tarp wall gets a corner on it: a second tarp clipped at a right angle off da fence post, 10 feet of front and 5 feet of side, both pegged on tomato stakes to stand 22 inches high. It covers one side all da way. Da open side, 90 degrees of wide-open approach, is where every hit comes from now, and da defenders spend da afternoon turnin’ to face it about 1 second too late." },
  "Wall and Moat": { label: "Wall and Moat", desc: "Da dirt for bankin’ da bottom of da tarp comes from a trench 2 feet deep and 3 wide out front now, and den da hose fills it, addin’ 2 feet of height from da attackin’ side. Da whole works run 14 feet across and take 90 minutes, about 70 of ’em waitin’ for da trench to fill at 9 gallons a minute. A straight charge has gotta wade da moat, which adds about 4 seconds out in da open and 1 lost sandal." },
  "Three-Sided Pool Fort": { label: "T’ree-Sided Pool Fort", desc: "T’ree tarp walls, a front and 2 sides, go up around da kiddie pool, about 8 by 6 feet inside and 2 feet high, wit’ da back left open toward da spigot for resupply. It’s da first fort anybody sits inside instead of behind, in 9 inches of water at 90°F. Holdin’ it is easy and leavin’ it is out of da question, and da defenders only get relieved by kids willin’ to get in." },
  "Fort Season": { label: "Fort Season", desc: "Forts go up for real now: tarp walls tied to lawn chairs at 30 inches high across 16 feet of front, wit’ 200 to 300 balloons cushioned in buckets of water behind ’em. Teams of 4 to 6 form up a side, and dey hash out rules on wall height, nozzle settings, refill times, and what ya can swing. Buildin’ runs 90 minutes before anybody t’rows, most of it spent tyin’." },
  "Lined Foxholes": { label: "Lined Foxholes", desc: "One-person pits 2 feet deep get dug out in front of da forts, lined wit’ a black garbage bag, and filled from da hose up to da guy’s waist. Each one takes 1 digger about 25 minutes and holds 15 gallons. Da guy in it is da coolest person in da whole war and can’t get out in a hurry, which da udder side finds out on da first charge." },
  "Fort-Side Pump Bay": { label: "Fort-Side Pump Bay", desc: "Forts start gettin’ built wit’ a covered bay in da back wall sized for a pump wit’ its 6 feet of intake hose coiled up. Goin’ from a spent bucket to a full one drops under 3 minutes ’cause nobody walks back to a spigot. Every fort plan drawn after dis has da bay, even a bunch dat never get a pump at all." },
  "Four-Tarp Fort": { label: "Four-Tarp Fort", desc: "Four tarp walls close da spot in all da way, about 8 by 8 feet inside and 30 inches high, wit’ a crawl-t’rough gap in da back wall. Da corners, where 2 tarps meet on one stake, are da weak spots: dey sag first, and a squirt aimed at a corner goes right t’rough about 1 time in 3. Da defenders end up sittin’ in da corners to hold ’em shut, which makes da corners da most-hit spots in da fort, eh." },
  "Fallback Tub": { label: "Fallback Tub", desc: "A second, smaller spot gets set up behind da main wall to fall back to: a galvanized stock tank 6 feet across and 2 feet deep, holdin’ a reserve of 80 balloons floatin’ in 100 gallons. It gets used twice in one afternoon, and both times da line gets took back inside 10 minutes. Da works on each side run past 30 feet of front now." },
  "Pump Crews": { label: "Pump Crews", desc: "Runnin’ da pump quits bein’ a one-man job: 3 to a pump, wit’ 1 on da handle, 1 at da source keepin’ da intake hose under da water and da strainer clear, and 1 haulin’ buckets. Steady output about triples over one guy alone, mostly ’cause nobody quits pumpin’ to fix da hose. Da crew is da first t’ing in da war set up around a piece of gear instead of around a fort or a buddy." },
  "Rival Forts": { label: "Rival Forts", desc: "Da udder side starts buildin’ across da yard at 40 to 60 feet away. Both sides put da front wall up full height, 3 feet of doubled tarp across 18 feet of front, and leave da sides open across a 60-degree approach. All told it runs 4 person-hours, and 2 trips to da hardware store for clips, before eider side fires anyt’ing." },
  "Ring Pool Fort": { label: "Ring Pool Fort", desc: "Da fix for saggy corners is a blow-up ring pool 9 feet across inside and 3 feet high, which got no corners at all and da same arc of fire every which way. Filled 2 feet deep it holds about 950 gallons, which takes near 2 hours at 9 gallons a minute. Da top ring gets blown up uneven by 3 different kids, so da wall stands 3 feet on da house side and 26 inches on da side dat matters, holy wah." },
  "Contested Lawn": { label: "Contested Lawn", desc: "Da 40 feet of open lawn between da forts changes hands 6 times in one afternoon at 99°F. Neider side holds it more dan 11 minutes, and every try costs about 40 balloons and starts wit’ a towel launcher layin’ 12 of ’em across da front. Da piles put togedder pass 600, and fillin’ keeps up wit’ t’rowin’ for da first time all summer." },
  "Deniable Rain": { label: "Deniable Rain", desc: "An overnight run pumps 60 gallons out of da stock tank t’rough a sprinkler head across 400 feet of fought-over ground, and da udder side wakes up sure it rained. Da trick holds most of a mornin’ and falls apart on da driveway, which is wet only in a perfect arc, and on da stock tank, which is down 3 inches. After dat both sides check da stock tank before dey believe any weather at all, eh." },
  "Sunken Pool Fort": { label: "Sunken Pool Fort", desc: "Da floor inside a ring wall gets dug 18 inches down and lined wit’ 2 tarps, so a 3-foot wall gives ya 4.5 feet of cover and da hose fills da pit up to a defender’s waist. It’s wall and trench all at once. Water pools in da floor just like it does in every dug-out spot, and for da first time in da history of any war, dat’s da whole point." },
  "The Filler’s Post": { label: "Da Filler’s Post", desc: "Walls go to doubled tarp on a frame of tomato stakes, 40 inches high across 20 feet of front, enough to take a balloon square on witout saggin’. One kid gets made da filler full time and works nonstop at da faucet adapter behind da line at a steady 12 balloons a minute, 4 of which go to da launcher crews every time. Da standin’ piles pass 500 a side." },
  "Pump Pickets": { label: "Pump Pickets", desc: "Pumps get posted at da edges of held ground to keep a strip of bare dirt wetted down to mud, so anybody crossin’ it leaves a footprint. Dat turns da pump from a supply machine into a lookout. A crossin’ gets caught dis way at 2 in da afternoon, by a sneaker print 11 inches long, and da fight dat follows takes in 11 people and no water dat wasn’t moved dere on purpose." },
  "Sprinkler Outposts": { label: "Sprinkler Outposts", desc: "Little outposts get pushed 20 feet out in front of da main wall to break up anybody comin’: 2 a side, each a 5-foot tarp screen wit’ its own sprinkler, manned by 1. Dey fall inside 4 minutes of a real push on average and get set back up overnight. Defended depth goes from 6 feet to about 30, which puts da main wall out past where a towel launcher does any good." },
  "Two-Room Tarp Fort": { label: "Two-Room Tarp Fort", desc: "A tarp wit’ a doorway splits da fort into 2 rooms: a firin’ room up front wit’ da squirt ports, and a back room wit’ 3 buckets of balloons, a cooler, and da only shade. It’s da first fort wit’ a wall inside it. Inside an hour da back room’s got 4 kids in it and da firin’ room’s got 1, and somebody draws up a rotation on a paper plate." },
  "Glacis Soaking": { label: "Glacis Soakin’", desc: "Da open lawn in front of da works gets soaked on purpose before a siege, to make it slick and so every footprint shows. Two pump crews put 60 gallons across 320 feet of approach in about 50 minutes, drawin’ from da fallback tub. Da attackers answer by comin’ up along da 1 strip da spray couldn’t reach, a 2-foot edge along da fence." },
  "The Siege Starts": { label: "Da Siege Starts", desc: "Neider fort can be took by a straight charge no more; 3 tries over 2 hours all bog down at da moat. So da launchers get pointed up, lobbin’ balloons over da wall instead of at it from 80 feet, da first fire da defenders can’t see comin’ till it busts in da back room. Da standoff gets settled by who’s got da bigger pile, not by any one charge. Both sides hold over 800 balloons and fill faster dan dey can t’row." },
  "Four-Sprinkler Star Fort": { label: "Four-Sprinkler Star Fort", desc: "Da square fort gets pointy bastions on every corner, 4 in all, each one wit’ a sprinkler set to sweep da face of da next wall over. Every way up to da bottom of a wall is under spray from a bastion next door now, and dere’s no dry ground anywhere inside 15 feet. It takes 2 hose splitters and 140 feet of hose, and it fits da siege just right." },
  "Standing Siege": { label: "Standin’ Siege", desc: "Da siege turns into just how t’ings are instead of somet’ing dat happens. Each fort holds 6 wit’ 3 in reserve, and da lawn in between goes unclaimed for stretches of 40 minutes at 101°F. Fillin’ and t’rowin’ even out near 900 balloons a side, and neider pile budges." },
  "Soaker Hose Ring": { label: "Soaker Hose Ring", desc: "A line gets laid clear around da udder side’s fort wit’out a break: 180 feet of soaker hose behind a 3-foot tarp screen, weepin’ water day and night. Buildin’ it takes 2 days and 9 kids, and da finished loop gets walked by 2 shooters workin’ opposite arcs. Resupply into da fort inside drops to 0, and da garrison’s down to 400 balloons and whatever’s left in dere own pool." },
  "The Pump Manual": { label: "Da Pump Manual", desc: "Everyt’ing dey learned gets wrote down in 22 hand-numbered pages: stroke counts by container, da right depth for an intake in a kiddie pool, da 9-second relief valve check, and da standin’ rule about da dog’s bowl. It gets copied by hand 4 times and every copy drifts. T’ree centuries later, 4 surviving copies disagree on da stroke table and all 4 point back to da same lost original, eh." },
  "Squirt Ports": { label: "Squirt Ports", desc: "Walls go up to 4 or 5 feet of doubled tarp on tomato stakes, wit’ 4 squirt ports a side cut 10 inches wide to clear a launcher’s pouch, and a lookout on a stepladder 2 feet over da wall. Ya can see out 100 feet, and da warnin’ on anybody comin’ goes from 3 seconds to 12. Da garrison gets up to 12 a fort." },
  "Night Pumping": { label: "Night Pumpin’", desc: "Pumpin’ moves to da dark ’cause ya can’t defend a kid bent over a handle at 30 strokes a minute in daylight. Da reflective tape on da handle and barrel, put on so nobody’d trip over a pump left in da yard and griped about at da time, turns out to be da only way crews find each udder. Output an hour drops by a quarter; gear lost drops to 0." },
  "Porch Watch Rotation": { label: "Porch Watch Rotation", desc: "Da spots get manned nonstop for da first time, in 2-hour shifts across 4 kids a fort. Coverage runs from 6 in da mornin’ to full dark at 9, about 15 hours, wit’ a loaded pistol kept at every squirt port da whole time. Two night raids get tried in a week by bunches of 3, both spotted inside 40 feet of da wall against 100 in daylight, and both run off inside 90 seconds." },
  "Waterfall Canopy": { label: "Waterfall Canopy", desc: "Da first roof: a 10-by-10-foot pop-up canopy wit’ a sprinkler zip-tied on top, so water sheets off all 4 edges in a curtain and da space underneat’ stays dry and 15°F cooler. It holds 3 sittin’, on a cooler and 2 lawn chairs. It takes 20 minutes to rig up and runs at 4 gallons a minute, and nobody inside can see out, which nobody inside minds, ya know." },
  "Pump Depots": { label: "Pump Depots", desc: "Pumps stop belongin’ to kids and start belongin’ to positions, kept in numbered coolers wit’ spare plunger washers, check valves, and hose clamps. Dere are 12 pumps and 40 spares across 4 depots, every one still da 1-inch bore and 6-inch stroke from da graph paper. Time from a split washer to a workin’ pump back at da line drops from a day to 11 minutes, eh." },
  "Three-Yard Campaign": { label: "Three-Yard Campaign", desc: "Tree or more strongholds now hold da block across 400 feet of frontage and 30 participants, at 99°F, ya know. Alliances form along lines of spigot access and harden da moment da first shared hose is run, holdin’ an average of 9 days apiece. No single yard can hold against da udder 2 togedder, and every yard knows it." },
  "Mist Dome": { label: "Mist Dome", desc: "A dome of 6 patio umbrellas lashed rib to rib over a filled kiddie pool, about 7 feet across inside and 5 feet tall, wit’ a mister ring at da crown. It does not work without roughly 150 gallons of standin’ water under it. Inside it holds 14°F cooler dan da yard, wit’ da hot air leavin’ t’rough a vent at da top and everyone enterin’ at a crouch t’rough a low tarp tunnel dat keeps da sun out, eh." },
  "Pump Repair Kit": { label: "Pump Repair Kit", desc: "A gallon zip bag goes out wit’ every pump crew, carryin’ 2 plunger washers, a spare check valve, 4 hose clamps, a roll of electrical tape, and a pocketknife, ya know. 90 percent of failures are now fixed where dey happen, usually in under 3 minutes. Da most-used item in da kit is da tape, and da least-used is da dowel handle, which nobody has ever seen fail." },
  "Lawn Works": { label: "Lawn Works", desc: "Construction spreads past da yards into da ground between dem: sandbag firin’ steps, tarp-lined drainage cuts, and mowed lanes totalin’ 300 feet of prepared position. Two lanes are laid out for a 3-person balloon launcher workin’ at 85 feet. Eleven people work it for a full day at 100°F and drink 9 gallons between dem. Holy wah, every approach to every yard now crosses at least 1 built obstacle, usually a wet one." },
  "Trench Bilge Pump": { label: "Trench Bilge Pump", desc: "A narrow pump is built to fit a trench floor, its intake cut down to a 1-inch foot, drawin’ off da standin’ water dat spillover and burst balloons leave in every low spot. At 2.6 ounces a stroke it clears a 3-foot puddle an inch deep in about 180 strokes. It does not help da fightin’ at all. It halves da falls, and a trench wit’ fewer falls holds more hours of occupation, which is da entire argument for it, eh." },
  "Trenches in the Lot": { label: "Trenches in da Lot", desc: "Connected firin’ positions are dug in da vacant lot at da end of da block, 2 feet deep and 60 to 90 feet in total run, ya know. Movement under cover becomes possible and da fightin’ goes below grade for da first time, at da cost of da 3-person launcher, which cannot be worked from 2 feet down. Transit between positions goes from an exposed 10 seconds to a covered 30. Da trenches begin collectin’ runoff by da second afternoon." },
  "Cooler Trench": { label: "Cooler Trench", desc: "A dedicated rear trench links every forward position to da cooler store, 120 feet of it at 3 feet deep. Refill runs dat previously drew fire now complete unobserved in 40 seconds, carryin’ 2 buckets each. Da sustainable rate of fire at da front line roughly doubles, and so does da mud, eh." },
  "Pump Sappers": { label: "Pump Sappers", desc: "Pump crews are attached directly to da diggin’ parties, wettin’ down fresh spoil so dat pale, dry dirt stops announcin’ new earthworks from across da lot, ya know. 40 feet of new trench is darkened to match da lawn in about 11 minutes, at roughly 300 strokes. Both sides now dig at night and pump by dawn, and neither can tell how much of da opposin’ line is new." },
  "Trench Stalemate": { label: "Trench Stalemate", desc: "Nobody stands upright in da open anymore, and overhand t’rowin’ gives way to a beach towel held by 2 people dat lobs balloons off da trench floor at 60 degrees. Da trench line runs 200 feet across both positions and every exchange comes from behind a lip of packed dirt, droppin’ hit rates from 1 in 4 to 1 in 11. An afternoon of fightin’ at 101°F moves da front about 6 feet, eh." },
  "Wet-Down Camouflage": { label: "Wet-Down Camouflage", desc: "Concealment becomes a discipline of its own: pump crews soak da ground around a position to da exact darkness of a sprinkled lawn, den flick extra water by hand so it does not read as pumped, ya know. A good crew hides a launcher emplacement in under 20 minutes and about 400 strokes. Da tell is always da edge, where pumped wet meets sprinkler wet at a line too straight to be a sprinkler. In 101°F sun da disguise lasts about 35 minutes." },
  "Culvert Network": { label: "Culvert Network", desc: "Passages link da trench system below ground t’rough da driveway culvert, 2 crawl spaces, and a dug run under da hedge, 3 feet down and 150 feet in aggregate length. Adults are informed once da network is complete, and da dug sections are inspected and shored wit’ fence pickets at 6-foot intervals. Holy wah, dere are 4 entrances, 2 of dem concealed and all of dem damp." },
  "Tunnel Pumps": { label: "Tunnel Pumps", desc: "Pumps go underground, drawin’ off da seepage dat has turned da culvert and crawl-space runs into 4 inches of standin’ water. Dey are worked by feel, in da dark, wit’ da hose coiled and da stroke shortened to 3 inches so da handle clears da ceiling. Two crews meet head-on in a passage neither knew was connected, and da resultin’ map is da most valuable document produced dat summer, eh." },
  "Skimmer-Pole Sling": { label: "Skimmer-Pole Sling", desc: "A pool skimmer pole is cut down to 4 feet, its net pocket loaded wit’ a balloon and swung two-handed from behind da wall, where dere is still room to stand, ya know. Range reaches 95 feet against a hand throw’s 70, and da release arc clears da barrier without exposin’ da t’rower. 4 are built and 2 are abandoned inside a week for want of anywhere safe to swing dem, and 1 pool owner wants da net back." },
  "Pool Undermining": { label: "Pool Undermining", desc: "A tunnel is driven under da opposin’ fence, 22 feet in 4 hours by 2 diggers workin’ in shifts, to da base of da inflatable pool behind it, and a tent stake goes up t’rough da floor. Da top ring sags and 8 feet of wall folds outward, dumpin’ about 300 gallons in 1.5 seconds. Holy wah, both sides begin listenin’ at da base of dere own pools each mornin’, which becomes standard practice." },
  "The Pump Guild": { label: "Da Pump Guild", desc: "Operators organize. Membership requires 10 clean strokes at 2.6 ounces apiece wit’ no weep at da elbow, a check-valve test performed from memory, and da standin’ rule recited word for word. Dere are 91 members in da first summer, of whom 14 are refused for pumpin’ across a kitchen floor and 1 for buildin’ a pump without a check valve, eh." },
  "Above-Ground Keep": { label: "Above-Ground Keep", desc: "A central stronghold wit’ a genuine interior: a 12-foot above-ground pool, 30 inches deep, under a tarp roof on tent poles, holdin’ about 2,100 gallons, ya know. Dere is 1 to an alliance rather dan 1 to a yard, both allied fences facin’ outward from it, and it is dim, buggy, drippin’ at about a drop a second from da tarp, and large enough to hold 4 people seated waist-deep around a cooler. Water temperature holds near 84°F regardless of da outside readin’." },
  "Keep Top-Off": { label: "Keep Top-Off", desc: "Da keep is put on a maintenance rotation: da waterline pumped back to da same marker line on da liner, da tarp rinsed, da deck boards wetted, all on a 3-day cycle by a standin’ crew of 6. At 2.6 ounces a stroke, da 1 inch lost to splash and evaporation each cycle takes about 3,500 strokes. Da keep never looks used. Visitors from outlyin’ yards report back dat da garrison must not be fightin’ at all, which is da intended readin’ and is wrong, eh." },
  "Inner Fence": { label: "Inner Fence", desc: "A second barrier is raised behind both allied fronts, enclosin’ da shared keep and da main cooler store: 60 feet of circuit in plywood and tarp, 5 feet high, braced every 30 inches, ya know. Takin’ either outer fence no longer ends anyt’ing. Two full assaults reach an outer line in a week, burn t’rough about 900 balloons, and neither gets inside da inner." },
  "Pump Registry": { label: "Pump Registry", desc: "Every pump in service is numbered in marker, logged, and assigned to a position, wit’ strokes pulled and gallons moved recorded per unit. Dere are 406 on da books, 11 cannot be found, and 1 turns up later in a sandbox. Da registry outlives da fortifications it was written to supply and becomes, much later, da oldest continuous record either side possesses, eh." },
  "Double Ring": { label: "Double Ring", desc: "A ring inside a ring: an outer wall of tarp-wrapped hay bales 3 feet high, and an inner platform of picnic tables 6 feet up so soakers can fire over da outer. Between dem runs an 8-foot moat of 4 sprinklers on a single valve. Holy wah, takin’ da outer ring only puts attackers in da wettest ground on da block, under fire from above, at a range of about 15 feet." },
  "Sheet-Armored Walls": { label: "Sheet-Armored Walls", desc: "Plastic sheetin’ is stapled over da walls, a 10-by-25-foot roll of 6-mil per side, and pulled taut overnight. Balloons now burst and run off rather dan soak in, cuttin’ effective damage by roughly two thirds, and both sides are sheetin’ within a day. Cardboard walls dat sagged after 1 afternoon now stand for 3, eh." },
  "Armored Pumps": { label: "Armored Pumps", desc: "Pumps worked under fire are given a bent cookie-sheet shield over da handle and a scrap of shower curtain over da operator, addin’ 4 pounds to a design built to weigh 1.5, ya know. Crew soakings during refills fall by two thirds. Output per hour falls by a fifth, since nobody can see da waterline anymore, and every crew offered da shield takes it." },
  "Sheeting Crews": { label: "Sheeting Crews", desc: "Sheetin’ becomes a standin’ assignment: 3 people per fort, 2 rolls of duct tape and 300 staples a night, patchin’ every puncture before dawn. Coverage extends from da front face to da full circuit, about 200 square feet re-tightened nightly. Unpatched sections are marked on da map and become da standard target, usually by 7 da next mornin’, eh." },
  "Pump Doctrine": { label: "Pump Doctrine", desc: "Pumpin’ gets a doctrine rather dan a manual: refill before da soakers run dry, never pump under observation, and never draw from da same puddle twice in a day, ya know. It is argued about for a generation and adopted almost unchanged. Da third rule exists because a puddle drawn twice leaves a pale ring, a puddle wit’ a ring reads as drawn, and a puddle dat reads as drawn is a target. It fits on 1 index card wit’ room for 2 more rules, which are never added." },
  "Tipping-Bucket Gate": { label: "Tipping-Bucket Gate", desc: "A gatehouse of plywood and pallets projects 8 feet out from da main gate, wit’ a dog-leg entry so no one can rush straight t’rough. Overhead hangs a 5-gallon bucket on a pivot, refilled by hose and tipped by a rope from inside. Holy wah, anyone who makes da turn takes about 40 pounds of water from 7 feet up, and da turn cannot be made at a run." },
  "Volley Ranks": { label: "Volley Ranks", desc: "T’rowers stop workin’ alone and are grouped into 2 ranks of 15, releasin’ balloons on a called count. A massed volley puts 30 rounds into a 20-foot square inside 2 seconds, which no individual arm has ever managed. Trainin’ to a usable standard takes 9 sessions, and about a third never reach it, eh." },
  "Pump Corps": { label: "Pump Corps", desc: "Pumpin’ becomes a trade wit’ its own crews: 9 operators in a line along da curb, drawin’ from da gutter runoff at da yard’s edge and feedin’ a single 40-foot hose header, ya know. Togedder dey refill a dry, contested position’s 2 coolers, about 25 gallons, in under 4 minutes at roughly 7 gallons a minute. T’rowers now draw from water da pumps recovered rather dan from da spigot, and da city stops being da only supplier." },
  "Picnic-Table Catapult": { label: "Picnic-Table Catapult", desc: "Da first catapult is assembled from a picnic-table bench, a 2-by-4, a salad bowl, and 4 bungee cords taken without permission and missed within a day. It launches a 6-inch balloon a measured 110 feet, roughly triple hand-t’row range, den comes apart on da second attempt. Holy wah, assembly took 2 hours and 5 people." },
  "Pump Liaison": { label: "Pump Liaison", desc: "A dedicated runner is attached between da pump crews and da launcher batteries so dat refillin’ and bombardment stop happenin’ in da same square at da same time. Before da liaison, 4 pumps are lost to friendly fire in a month, 1 of dem hit square on da handle by a balloon from 110 feet. After it, none, and da runner is da only person in da sector permitted to stop a battery mid-load, eh." },
  "Bottle Ballista": { label: "Bottle Ballista", desc: "A flat-trajectory engine built from a 2-liter bottle a third full of water, a bicycle pump, and 2 fence uprights, aimed rather dan lobbed, ya know. Pumped to 60 psi and released, it sends da bottle, sprayin’, t’rough a gap in da fence at 130 feet, wit’ 1 shot in 4 landin’ inside a 2-foot circle. Refill and re-pump take 50 seconds, which is its whole limitation." },
  "Lifeguard Tower": { label: "Lifeguard Tower", desc: "A freestandin’ tower of stacked pallets rises 12 feet, 6 feet square at da base and 3 at da top, wit’ a lifeguard chair bolted to da platform. It is useless as a firin’ position past 40 feet and invaluable as a lookout, since every launcher on da block can be seen from it. A spotter wit’ a whistle calls corrections for 2 batteries, 1 blast per 10 feet short, eh." },
  "Counter-Battery": { label: "Counter-Battery", desc: "Engines are now built specifically to soak da crews and burst da stockpiles of udder engines, at ranges out to 140 feet, ya know. Four are in service across da block and 3 are disabled within a week, 2 of dem by udder engines. Median operational life of a launcher falls to 9 shots." },
  "Pump-and-Fill Cycle": { label: "Pump-and-Fill Cycle", desc: "Da two trades are formally interlocked: a pump crew draws a fresh barrel from da shaded cistern, fill crews load balloons from it within da hour while it is still near 70°F, and da empty barrel goes back into da rotation. Round quality rises measurably, because a balloon of 70°F water lands as an event, while one filled from hose water dat sat at 110°F lands as a bath. Output per participant per day roughly doubles and does not fall again, eh." },
  "Balloon Trebuchet": { label: "Balloon Trebuchet", desc: "A counterweight engine wit’ a pivotin’ arm and a 5-gallon bucket of water for mass, t’rowin’ a balloon 260 feet against da 140 of anyt’ing else in service. Construction takes 9 hours and a pit dug to seat da frame. It outranges every counter-battery engine on da block, which ends da counter-battery advantage in a day. Holy wah, da counterweight is drained and refilled each evenin’, because it is technically also ammunition." },
  "Balloon Plant": { label: "Balloon Plant", desc: "A dedicated fill line runs in a garage wit’ a foreman and 6 workers on 3 splitter manifolds, ya know. Output runs 600 to 900 balloons an hour, standin’ inventory passes 2,000, and every round is checked for a 6-inch diameter, a tight knot, and a fit in da launcher cups before it goes into a laundry basket. Rejection rate settles around 1 in 8, and rejects are t’rown at da foreman’s discretion." },
  "Recovery Line": { label: "Recovery Line", desc: "Da balloon plant gains a matched pump line runnin’ da opposite direction: one crew fills balloons, da udder pumps da runoff from da low corner of da yard back into da fill tank so da fillers never run dry. Throughput settles near 70 gallons an hour recovered against 1,900 balloons filled. Da two lines are timed against each udder on a whiteboard by da garage door, and for da first week da pumps are winnin’, eh." },
  "Launcher Shops": { label: "Launcher Shops", desc: "Launchers stop being built where dey are used: 2 shops are set up in garages behind da line, holdin’ bench seats, arms, bowls, and cut surgical tubin’, ya know. Build time falls from 9 hours to 3, and a wrecked launcher is rebuilt rather dan written off. Standin’ spares run to 4 complete frames and 60 feet of tubin’." },
  "Pump Foundry": { label: "Pump Foundry", desc: "A works is set up to mold barrels and plungers rather dan cut dem from PVC, wit’ seals in nitrile chosen because natural rubber cracks in da sun and da sun is da entire operatin’ condition. Output goes from 4 pumps a week to 60, every one still a 1-inch bore wit’ a 6-inch stroke. Da material decision is made once, by someone who leaves 2 samples on a car dashboard at 160°F for a week, and is never revisited, eh." },
  "Draw Sites": { label: "Draw Sites", desc: "Cool, clean water becomes da constraint rather dan labor, and dedicated draw sites are opened away from da fightin’: a creek pool, 2 rain-barrel banks, and an abandoned koi pond, ya know. Each yields about 600 balloons a day, a fifth of it clean enough to run t’rough da fill nozzles without cloggin’. Haul distance averages 300 feet. Two of da 4 are contested within da first week of da 104°F heat wave and change hands 3 times between dem." },
  "Barrel Casemates": { label: "Barrel Casemates", desc: "Da walls are now thick enough to hollow out: a double row of filled 55-gallon drums, roofed wit’ plywood, wit’ firin’ chambers built inside da thickness of da wall itself. Each chamber seats 2 soakers firin’ t’rough a 6-inch slot under full shade, and runs about 15°F cooler dan da open wall top. Holy wah, a direct hit from a trebuchet now lands on 460 pounds of drum and changes nuttin’." },
  "Ranging Flags": { label: "Ranging Flags", desc: "Fire stops being guesswork: orange utility flags are pushed in at 25-foot intervals out to 200 feet, and every engine is walked onto its target wit’ 3 rangin’ shots. Hit rate at maximum range rises from 1 in 11 to 1 in 4. A rangin’ book is kept per engine and copied whenever crews rotate, and 1 of da flags turns out to mark an actual gas line, eh." },
  "Bore Standardization": { label: "Bore Standardization", desc: "Every pump in service is brought to one pattern, a 1-inch bore, a 6-inch stroke, and 2.6 ounces a pull, so dat any plunger fits any barrel anywhere on da front, ya know. Dere are 900 non-standard pumps recalled and re-sleeved. Da bore chosen is da one drawn on graph paper at 4 squares to da inch before a single drop had been t’rown, unchanged, and nobody on da front remembers who argued for it." },
  "The Four-Block Front": { label: "Da Four-Block Front", desc: "Da conflict has outgrown a single block and runs across 4, roughly 2,000 feet of contested frontage in a 105°F heat wave. Da main wall is upgraded to stacked water drums and plywood 6 feet high, 3 feet thick, and 220 feet long, 1 to each two-yard alliance; 3 alliances put 660 feet of da frontage behind wall. Territory is mapped on notebook paper by 1 person per side and revised about 9 times a day. Participation passes 60, and launcher density settles at 1 per 400 feet of frontage, eh." },
  "Pump Reconnaissance": { label: "Pump Reconnaissance", desc: "Pump crews are sent forward specifically to drain puddles and come back reportin’ what dey saw, which is more dan any scout gets, because a pump crew has a reason to be kneelin’ for 20 minutes in open ground, ya know. 4 sectors are mapped dis way in a week. Both sides work out what is happenin’ at about da same time and start readin’ each other’s drained puddles as intelligence, at roughly 1 dry ring per visit." },
  "Triangle Outworks": { label: "Triangle Outworks", desc: "Detached triangular outworks go up 30 feet in front of da main wall, each a V of sandbags 12 feet to a side wit’ 2 sprinklers inside da point. Any attack on da wall now has to split around dem, and anyone who goes between 2 of dem is under fire from both. Holy wah, dere are 5 along da front, and 1 is overrun on da first day and turned around to face da wall it was protectin’." },
  "Salients": { label: "Salients", desc: "Da line stops being straight. Tree bulges push into opposin’ territory at depths of 60 to 100 feet, each defended on 3 sides across 180 feet of wall and each needin’ about 40 percent more people per foot of frontage. Each is held by 2 launchers firin’ on divergent arcs and supplied by a single hose run, and 2 of da 3 are given up voluntarily within a week, eh." },
  "Counter-Pump Fire": { label: "Counter-Pump Fire", desc: "Batteries begin rangin’ on pump crews as a target class of dere own, on da reasonin’ dat a front which cannot be refilled cannot be held, ya know. It is da first time in da war dat da equipment is valued above da position. Pump losses in da first month run to 31, mostly cracked barrels and 1 handle lost down a storm drain, and every one is replaced from da foundry inside a week." },
  "Bombardment": { label: "Bombardment", desc: "Indirect fire becomes continuous rather dan occasional, at a sustained 40 balloons an hour per engine across 9 engines. Nothin’ is aimed at a person; da target is da wall itself, and sheeted frontage dat once lasted a week now lasts 2 days. Holy wah, both sides begin patchin’ under fire as a standin’ task, and da lawn between da lines stays wet for 11 straight days at 106°F." },
  "Wagon Supply Lines": { label: "Wagon Supply Lines", desc: "Water must be moved to where it is needed over hauls of 500 feet or more. Wagons are requisitioned at 2 per side from 4 households dat are not asked, 1 of dem reserved for counterweight water, routes are fixed, and da delivery rate, about 400 balloons an hour, sets da pace of da entire war. Forward positions hold 90 minutes of supply in da sun before da stock warms past use, eh." },
  "Pump Convoys": { label: "Pump Convoys", desc: "Resupply goes mobile, ya know. Pumps are lashed to wagons and run forward along da supply lines demselves, drawin’ from curb gutters and storm-drain inlets to refill positions under fire. A convoy of 6 works 2 miles of curb in 4 hours. Each pump carries its own 4-liter primin’ jug for da stretches where da gutter is dry, and doctrine reclassifies da pump as a supply engine rather dan a weapon." },
  "Clothesline Railhead": { label: "Clothesline Railhead", desc: "A clothesline trolley is strung from a second-floor window to within 400 feet of da line, carryin’ pumps and dere primin’ jugs forward without anyone pullin’ a wagon. 60 pumps a day arrive at da end of da line. It is cut twice, restrung twice, and da second restringin’ moves it 200 feet further back, which nobody objects to, eh." },
  "Canopy Blockhouses": { label: "Canopy Blockhouses", desc: "Pop-up canopies wit’ tarp walls and a cooler apiece are posted every 200 feet along da supply routes, each a garrison of 3 wit’ 2 soakers and a lawn chair, ya know. Dey guard da wagons and da clothesline railhead and run about 20°F cooler dan da open route. Convoy ambushes along da chain fall from 4 a day to 1, and da canopies become da most requested postin’ in da war." },
  "Forward Coolers": { label: "Forward Coolers", desc: "Forward stores are set up behind each sector, 6 of dem holdin’ 1,500 balloons apiece in ice chests under wet towels. Time from request to delivery at da line drops from 25 minutes to 4. Holy wah, two depots sit inside trebuchet range of da opposin’ works and are moved after da first one is hit, at a loss of 1,100 balloons and 1 cooler lid." },
  "Trailer Pumps": { label: "Trailer Pumps", desc: "Da 12-inch standard pump gets mounted on a 2-axle trailer and towed along da canal bank behind a pickup, its suction hose trailin’ in da water and an eccentric off da rear axle workin’ da plunger 3 times every wheel turn. Da plunger moves only when da wheels move, which keeps da original behavior just like it was: no pull, no water. At a walkin’ pace one unit moves about 3,500 gallons a minute, 10 times what 2 grown-ups on da handle could manage, and lays a wet stripe 12 feet wide across da bank. It takes 4 people to stop it, eh." },
  "Roving Deck Guns": { label: "Roamin’ Deck Guns", desc: "Borrowed deck guns get bolted onto flatbeds and moved every night, 4 to a battery, so no hydrant gets used twice. Movin’ takes 20 minutes and cuts shutoffs by da water department by t’ree quarters, since nobody can shut off a hydrant dey can’t find. Da catch is aim: da first burst from a new spot lands 60 feet wide on average, usually on somebody’s parked car." },
  "Forward Soak Lines": { label: "Forward Soak Lines", desc: "Everyt’ing in front of da citadel gets rebuilt to da new scale: 4 sprinkler outworks a side, each 16 feet of perforated hose on a berm 4 feet high, pushed 60 feet out, and a redoubt made from an 8-foot stock tank holdin’ 800 gallons. Da old soaker lines get relaid wit’ ’em at 900 feet against da original 300, by 31 people over 3 days, wit’ da lanes widened to take deck guns. Defended depth from da farthest sprinkler to da citadel wall reaches 240 feet, just inside da reach of da heaviest monitor dat can get dere." },
  "Natatorium Citadel": { label: "Natatorium Citadel", desc: "Da war’s first fortress wit’ a roof dat holds is da closed-up city natatorium: 5 rooms, countin’ a pool hall 40 feet on its longest side, under 18 feet of ceilin’, behind brick walls 3 feet thick. It gets a name, painted over da entrance, and used as da reference point for every position on da map. Takin’ it costs 60 person-hours, most of ’em spent findin’ da right key. An outer ring of sandbags 160 feet around and 7 feet high closes in 1,600 square feet of yard and water store. Da garrison runs 28 at da complex against 88 in da whole war." },
  "Citadel Wetting": { label: "Citadel Wettin’", desc: "Da whole natatorium gets kept under a maintained soak: 11 crews on rotation workin’ standard pumps, every wall, roof, and bit of yard rewetted inside 2 hours of dryin’. Usage runs about 22,000 gallons a day, roughly 630 strokes at 35 gallons, all of it pulled outta da pool and drained right back in, none of it new. From da outworks da citadel looks permanently, impossibly, fresh rained on in a 108°F drought, and everybody inside understands dat’s a political statement, ya know." },
  "Heat Conscription": { label: "Heat Conscription", desc: "Showin’ up stops bein’ voluntary. Every household across 4 blocks gets assessed at 1 body per 2 kids, makin’ a roll of 140. Exemptions go out for heat rash, for anybody wit’ a workin’ window air conditioner, and for da 11 crews already servin’ deck guns. Compliance runs about 70 percent, droppin’ to 40 after 2 in da afternoon." },
  "Refinery Monitors": { label: "Refinery Monitors", desc: "Two industrial fire monitors get borrowed from a refinery brigade, each rated at 2,000 gallons a minute and fed by a 5-inch supply hose dat takes a crew of 9 to move. Range reaches 300 feet, and one burst flattens a sprinkler outwork and strips da shade canopy off whatever’s behind it. Each one needs its own hydrant and a pumper truck, and ties up its crew for a full day every time it moves." },
  "Rash Guard Army": { label: "Rash Guard Army", desc: "Showin’ up stops bein’ casual at a roster of 90 across both sides. Dere’s 4 ranks, uniforms made outta matchin’ rash guards, and a chain of command dat runs every order t’rough 1 person under 1 umbrella. Attendance gets tracked daily, and turnout holds over 80 percent for 11 straight days of 105°F." },
  "Pump Conscription": { label: "Pump Conscription", desc: "Pump duty stops bein’ volunteered and starts bein’ assigned, at 400 crews called up in a season. Da pump guild objects dat an untrained crew short-strokes da 72-inch handle and loses prime, gets overruled, and gets proven right inside a month when average delivery variation goes from 14 percent to 31. Green recruits get measured pullin’ 40 inches of stroke and loggin’ it as 35 gallons. Trainin’ comes back, from da same bosses, wit’out a word about it." },
  "Rain Barrel Pillboxes": { label: "Rain Barrel Pillboxes", desc: "Da standin’ army digs in behind low firin’ posts made outta 55-gallon rain barrels sunk 6 inches into da lawn and capped wit’ domed lids, so each one stands about 3 feet above grade. Each has 2 slits 2 inches high, holds 1 kid and 1 squirt cannon, and keeps 10 gallons in da bottom for ammo and a foot bath both. Dey get built by da hundred: 340 in 3 weeks across da front, in rows da utility’s aerial survey logs as some new kinda crop." },
  "The Dry Officers": { label: "Da Dry Officers", desc: "Command gets split off from fightin’: 11 officers across both sides, and not one of ’em’s been wet in 9 days. Orders get written down instead of hollered, about 30 a day, a third of ’em deck gun instructions, and get carried by runners who are wet da whole time. Time from decidin’ to doin’ at da front runs 6 minutes." },
  "Pump Clauses": { label: "Pump Clauses", desc: "Settlements start havin’ terms about pumps in ’em: how many 12-inch standard units each side can hold, set at 12, which ground can get wetted, and a standin’ ban on pumpin’ where da udder side’s works can see ya. Every pump’s supposed to get logged by serial number and by stroke, at 35 gallons apiece. Da clauses are more detailed dan da ones about t’rowin’. Both sides stick to da count, and neider one sticks to da sight line." },
  "Pool Deck Accords": { label: "Pool Deck Accords", desc: "Formal agreements get drafted and signed on da backs of laminated pool schedules by delegations of 6. Dey set boundaries to da foot, water limits at 3,000 gallons a side a day, a ceilin’ of 4 deck guns apiece, and terms for startin’ back up after da 4 o’clock adult swim. Four documents are in force at once, and 2 of ’em fight over da same curb." },
  "Over-the-Roof Fire": { label: "Over-da-Roof Fire", desc: "Deck guns get aimed at targets da crews can’t see, lobbin’ water over 2-story rooftops on corrections hollered down by a spotter up on da water tower catwalk. Spottin’ to correction runs 90 seconds, and da hit rate on a backyard ya can’t see settles near 1 in 7. Da back of every house is a target for da first time, and 3 households find out dere gutters work." },
  "Curb Line Commission": { label: "Curb Line Commission", desc: "A standin’ body of 9 gets seated to figure out what da accords mean, split even between both sides plus 1 neutral from da library. It rules on 41 disputes in a month, and folks accept its findin’s in 34. Boundary markers, blue dots of utility paint, go on da curb every 50 feet across 2 miles." },
  "Rain Falsification": { label: "Rain Falsification", desc: "Pumpin’ gets used big to fake a rainstorm dat never happened, across 9 square miles, to justify a rain-day closin’ of summer school in a district dat got nothin’. Some 600 standard pumps work all night at 35 gallons a stroke, soakin’ every gauge, gutter, and street, about 41 million gallons all told. It works. Da record for dat date reads as a 4-inch storm in every account written locally and as a dry day in every satellite pass, and folks on both sides still cite da difference 1,000 years later for opposite reasons." },
  "The Median Line": { label: "Da Median Line", desc: "Da war’s first continuous fortified line runs 6.2 miles down da boulevard median and gets named for da utility director, who won’t say nothin’ about it. Rain barrel pillboxes stand every 40 feet, hooked togedder by buried soaker hose, wit’ 9 sandbagged casemates for deck guns and 3 storm-drain tunnels for movin’ crews where nobody can see. It holds 800 people and goes t’rough 90,000 gallons a day. It faces east, and da first serious push comes around da sout’ end t’rough a strip mall parkin’ lot." },
  "Total Water War": { label: "Total Water War", desc: "A regional campaign spreads over 7 neighborhoods, wit’ coordinated pushes on a shared timetable. Da war’s outlasted da heat wave dat started it by 9 days and pulls in upwards of 300 folks. Daily usage across all fronts goes past 1.2 million gallons, a third of it t’rown by battery: 40 guns in service, deck guns and refinery monitors in about even numbers." },
  "Pattern Cannons": { label: "Pattern Cannons", desc: "Cannon buildin’ gets standardized to one drawin’, 1 barrel length, 1 nozzle tip, and 1 hydrant couplin’, so parts off any wreck fit any survivor. Ninety get built to da pattern in a month, against 40 of mixed design still in service. Time to get a busted cannon back on da line drops from 2 days to 4 hours." },
  "All Hands to the Hydrants": { label: "All Hands to Da Hydrants", desc: "Every eligible body across da region gets called up at once, 1,100 of ’em inside 48 hours. Production goes all in on ammo at 40,000 filled balloons a day, and every garden spigot in 4 districts gets a quota. Summer school attendance across dose 4 districts drops under half and nobody records it dat week." },
  "Pump Divisions": { label: "Pump Divisions", desc: "Pumpin’ gets its own command structure: 4 divisions of 900 standard pumps each, rankin’ right alongside da batteries instead of under da supply train. One synchronized stroke of all 3,600 would move 126,000 gallons, which nobody’s ever once tried. Da change gets fought for 2 seasons and settled by one number: fronts wit’ a division attached hold 3 times as long as fronts wit’out one. From here on out, pump officers get saluted." },
  "Bicycle Blitz": { label: "Bicycle Blitz", desc: "Piles of ammo and speed push fortification right out. Positions get abandoned mid-campaign for fast columns of 11 on bicycles, each towin’ a wagon of 400 balloons and a 3-person launcher, coverin’ a quarter mile in under 2 minutes. Average fight length drops from 50 minutes to 9, dey get 600 feet past da line, and da front moves up a block a day." },
  "Pump-Ahead Doctrine": { label: "Pump-Ahead Doctrine", desc: "Doctrine flips. Instead of rewettin’ ground dey already hold, columns of trailer pumps run out ahead of da advance, fillin’ stock tanks and kiddie pools on ground nobody’s taken yet, so da attack shows up at refill spots dat are already full. Each trailer moves about 3,500 gallons a minute at a walkin’ pace. Fronts move 11 miles in a week for da first time in da war, and losses in da forward pumps, mostly impounded, run over half; dey keep da doctrine anyhow." },
  "Spigot Raiders": { label: "Spigot Raiders", desc: "Parties of 4 carryin’ 60 balloons apiece work up to 2 miles behind da udder side’s line for as long as 3 days, movin’ 9 miles a night. Dey go after refill stations, ice depots, and deck gun parks instead of positions; 9 get knocked out in a month, mostly by pullin’ da spigot handles off, at an estimated cost of 60,000 gallons. Only 2 raids in 5 reach a target at all, and about a third of da parties miss dere ride home, usually at somebody’s pool." },
  "Computed Spray": { label: "Computed Spray", desc: "Firin’ gets figured instead of eyeballed: range, nozzle angle, hydrant pressure, and wind all get worked out on paper before da first burst. Four deck guns firin’ 1 solution land inside a 9-foot circle at 200 feet. A battery clerk gets appointed, and every range book gets boiled down to one table, laminated against da splash." },
  "Clearwell Bunker": { label: "Clearwell Bunker", desc: "Da command goes all da way underground, into da shut-down clearwell of da old waterworks, 20 feet down and 18°F cooler dan da street, reached by 2 manhole shafts. Its empty 1-million-gallon basin holds 40 staff, a map table, and da only shade in da war ya can count on. Up top is a decoy field of 60 dummy sprinklers on timers, which pulls in 3 raids its first week." },
  "Fortress Schools": { label: "Fortress Schools", desc: "Strongholds big enough to put up dere own garrisons overnight get made outta da 3 grade schools wit’ workin’ air conditioning, 40 to 60 folks apiece in 41 rooms. Utility tunnels hook boiler rooms up to more rooms dey’re still explorin’, past 900 feet of mapped passage and nobody knows how much more. Each one’s ringed by 540 feet of sandbagged sprinkler line 9 feet high, closin’ in 18,000 square feet of buildin’, store, and playground. Da network’s got 19 entrances, 7 of ’em hidden and 2 of ’em da cafeteria." },
  "City-Scale Pumps": { label: "City-Size Pumps", desc: "Da standard pump gets scaled up again by 4, keepin’ da proportions exact: a 48-inch bore and a 288-inch stroke, mounted on a flatbed and run off its rear axle. Each stroke moves about 2,240 gallons, and one unit wets 11 blocks a shift on a nightly schedule. Folks quit tellin’ rain from maintenance, and in 3 neighborhoods da word for a shower turns into da word for da truck." },
  "Summer Staff College": { label: "Summer Staff College", desc: "Plannin’ turns into somet’ing dey teach. Twenty-two candidates a year study supply, frontage, pressure, and da 9 campaigns fought so far, in a course dat runs 4 months, which is to say da whole summer. Graduates plan 3 weeks out instead of one afternoon, and campaigns run about twice as long." },
  "Splash Academy": { label: "Splash Academy", desc: "Technique gets taught proper to newcomers over a 6-session curriculum. Dere’s a right way to tie a balloon, 2 wraps and 1 tuck in 3 seconds, a right way to t’row, a 4-person drill for chargin’ and aimin’ a deck gun, and a written-down order for pullin’ back under spray. Roughly 40 students sign up each intake, and 80 percent graduate." },
  "The Pump Academy": { label: "Da Pump Academy", desc: "A school gets started dat teaches nothin’ but pumpin’: stroke tables, primin’, how da check valve behaves, and 400 hours on da handle. Da entrance exam is one unbroken run of 100 full strokes, judged by how da valve sounds. Da curriculum opens wit’ da graph-paper sketch at 4 squares to da inch, copied from a copy of a copy, da arrow labeled “water comes out here” now pointin’ a little bit at da handle." },
  "Pressure Zones": { label: "Pressure Zones", desc: "Territory gets split into 11 pressure zones, each wit’ its own reservoir, pump station, deck gun park, and roll of about 200. Orders from da center reach da farthest zone in a day and a half. T’ree zones make more dan dey use and get taxed a fift’ of output, about 9,000 gallons a week apiece, brought by tanker every Thursday." },
  "Imperial Pump Works": { label: "Imperial Pump Works", desc: "Da empire turns pumpin’ into industry. Forty pump works across da zones crank out unit after unit on one pattern, cast in a bronze dat won’t gall at 110°F so da plungers never seize up in August, and field a standin’ park of 1,100 pumps. Whole fronts get rewetted overnight, and da phrase “out of water” quietly drops outta da language. Da works get guarded heavier dan da deck guns dey supply." },
  "The Imperial Gauge": { label: "Da Imperial Gauge", desc: "One standard gets decreed for every work in da empire, set on purpose above da biggest one already standin’ so no position meets it and every position has to grow. Moats: 6 feet deep and 4 across da floor. Reserve tanks: 22 feet across and 9 high, about 25,000 gallons. Main sprinkler wall: 550 feet long, t’rowin’ a curtain 14 feet high at 80 psi, wit’ a spray tower every 100 feet. Surveyors go out wit’ a pressure gauge and a bound book and report dat not one work meets da standard, which is da whole point." },
  "The Great Repiping": { label: "Da Great Repipin’", desc: "Every position in da empire gets enlarged to da gauge in 1 season: 22 settlements, an estimated 18 miles of supply line and 9 of moat dug wider and deeper, by 60,000 hands workin’ outta da same book. Every hydrant couplin’ gets rethreaded to 1 standard, so a hose charged in one zone seats on any outlet in any udder. Two positions say no and keep dere inherited threads; both get abandoned inside da year, not by any order but because nothin’ else in da empire screws onto ’em. Da word for a t’ing built to no standard turns into da word for dose 2 positions." },
  "Water Empire": { label: "Water Empire", desc: "Da works can be seen from an airliner now: 2.5 miles of continuous sprinkler wall t’rowin’ a 14-foot curtain, run from a capital dat wrote da gauge and built past it, wit’ a main wall 1,650 feet long and reserve tanks holdin’ 75,000 gallons. Outlyin’ positions pay tribute at 20,000 gallons of chilled water a week and get deck gun support back. Twenty-two settlements are signed up, 2 are behind on payments and keep dere support anyhow, and 4 more are still dickerin’." },
  "The Frontier Curtain": { label: "Da Frontier Curtain", desc: "Da empire’s border gets fixed by one long curtain of spray along da city limit, 23 miles long, built to da imperial gauge at 14 feet high and 80 psi. Dere’s a pump house every mile, each movin’ 400 gallons a minute, wit’ 2 spray turrets between each pair. Da curtain loses about a third of its water to da air before it lands, which cools da frontier by 9°F and makes it da most popular postin’ in da empire. Smugglers cross it wet, eh." },
  "The Pump Ministry": { label: "Da Pump Ministry", desc: "Pumpin’ turns into a department of state wit’ a budget bigger dan da batteries’, 4,000 staff, and say over 11,000 standard pumps at 35 gallons a stroke and over which ground in da empire gets wetted and when. It answers to nobody who ever pulled a handle. Its first published act is a schedule of waterin’ priorities where fortifications rank below street trees, which kicks off a constitutional crisis and stands anyhow." },
  "The Air-Conditioned Court": { label: "Da Air-Conditioned Court", desc: "Decisions move inside, into da only rooms kept at 68°F. Nine advisors crowd around da commander, not one of ’em holdin’ a position on any river, and 4 of da last 6 big appointments went to folks who never got wet in da war. Two field commands change hands in a month for reasons nobody writes down. Heat at dis tier is a historic heat dome, 116°F in a city built for 80, and da court don’t go outside to check." },
  "The Dam Palace": { label: "Da Dam Palace", desc: "Da court moves into a concrete dam 210 feet high and fortifies it mostly against its own barons. Wards get handed out by rank and by depth, da lowest galleries holdin’ 58°F down in da concrete, and each lower ward can only be reached t’rough da one above it. Da throne hall sits under da dome of da intake tower, 40 feet across, where da hum of 6 turbines makes whisperin’ impossible and plottin’ a real pain." },
  "Water Barons": { label: "Water Barons", desc: "Control of da water starts passin’ to heirs by birth instead of merit, across 11 separate lines, each one holdin’ a right dated by its filin’. Authority gets fixed by priority date and holds about 4 days before da first claim gets contested. A hereditary baron lasts 31 hours on average before gettin’ replaced, usually by a cousin wit’ an older piece of paper." },
  "Downstream Kingdoms": { label: "Downstream Kingdoms", desc: "Nine smaller outfits keep dere own flags, calendars, and inside law while handin’ over all da upstream dealin’. Each one sends 300 bodies when asked and gets protection, a fixed allowance of 4,000 acre-feet a season, and 6 standard pumps it can’t sell off. Two try to renegotiate in da same dry season, and 1 pulls it off." },
  "The Pump Cartels": { label: "Da Pump Cartels", desc: "Manufacturin’ bunches up into 4 houses holdin’ da castin’ patterns between ’em, settin’ price, pattern, and delivery for every pump in da alliance. A plunger seal, which is still pretty much a trimmed faucet washer, costs 11 times what it did under da old works and fails just as often. Two downstream kingdoms try castin’ dere own 12-inch cylinders, get cut off from seals, and cave inside a season." },
  "Basin War": { label: "Basin War", desc: "Separate cities’ water wars run into each udder along da same river and merge into one fight spannin’ 400 miles of basin. Nobody in it can name every side; da count’s at 19 and still climbin’, and some get counted on 2 sides at once because dey’re upstream of one enemy and downstream of anudder. Total folks in it run an estimated 11,000." },
  "Cannon Doctrine": { label: "Cannon Doctrine", desc: "Written doctrine covers da cannons demselves: when to bunch ’em up, when to hold ’em back, and how much of da water dey get to use. It sets da ratio at 1 cannon per 40 folks and a third of all water t’rown. T’ree of da 19 sides refuse it and keep doin’ it dere own way, which is mostly buckets." },
  "Watershed Councils": { label: "Watershed Councils", desc: "Assemblies get called to settle questions of practice instead of territory: how cold t’rown water’s allowed to be, where frozen balloons stand, and how old ya gotta be to fight. Four councils sit in 2 years, 1 of ’em for 11 weeks in a hotel ballroom kept at 71°F. Not one of da 4 rulin’s gets accepted everywhere." },
  "The Ice Schism": { label: "Da Ice Schism", desc: "Da war splits along doctrine lines over whedder ice-cored balloons are allowed, a question dat only came up once launchers started t’rowin’ ’em far enough to matter. Each side bans da udder’s practice and enforces it at home, wit’ cooler inspections at every checkpoint. Da split runs t’rough 31 settlements and cuts 14 of ’em right down da middle." },
  "The Pump Schism": { label: "Da Pump Schism", desc: "Da split runs right t’rough da pump. One tradition says pumped water is water and ya can fight over it like any udder; da udder says only rain dat fell is legit and anyt’ing dat went t’rough a bore is a forgery. Samples from both sides match to 4 decimal places, which is why it drags on. Both sides keep pumpin’ at 35 gallons a stroke, and both swear da udder side’s soakin’s don’t count." },
  "The Water Code": { label: "Da Water Code", desc: "Written law takes over from custom: 412 articles coverin’ well rights, canal access, and where ya stand if ya never got wet. Da first article is first in time, first in right. Courts sit in 9 settlements and hear about 200 cases a year, a fift’ of ’em about property instead of fightin’." },
  "Water Civilization": { label: "Water Civilization", desc: "Permanent buildin’s, permanent institutions, and over 4,000 folks livin’ in each settlement. Fightin’ takes up under a fift’ of da work now; da rest goes to movin’ water, food, mappin’, and upkeep. Ninety percent of da standin’ structures, every canal too, are older dan dis campaign, and 2 of da aqueducts are older dan da war." },
  "Pumphouse Monastery": { label: "Pumphouse Monastery", desc: "Da pump liturgy and canon get kept and guarded in a walled cloister built around an old brick pumphouse, 3 acres inside walls 12 feet high. Da scriptorium sits in da domed cistern underneat’, 52°F all year, where 40 monks copy da drawin’ at 4 squares to da inch, 1 sheet a day. Da monks are da garrison too, and each one keeps a workin’ 1-inch pump at da foot of da bed." },
  "Pump Liturgy": { label: "Pump Liturgy", desc: "Da first pull of da season turns into a formal occasion: a 1-inch pump gets primed, da handle gets drawn to its full 6-inch stroke, exactly 3 strokes get delivered at 2.6 ounces each, and da check valve gets listened to out loud in words nobody can translate anymore. Da phrase in da middle of it is an 800-year mangle of “no leak at da elbow.” Both sides of da schism do it exactly da same." },
  "The Recited Logs": { label: "Da Recited Logs", desc: "A fixed body of recited stuff forms: roughly 200 pieces, none written down, held by about 40 specialists, lotsa dem pressure logs from a water tower nobody can find. Recitin’ da whole canon takes 11 hours and gets done twice a year, in da shade. Checkin’ 2 far-apart reciters shows dey agree on 94 percent of da lines and on every number." },
  "The First Drop": { label: "Da First Drop", desc: "Origin stories get told about da first water ever t’rown, in at least 8 competin’ versions: a flicked fingertip, an ice cube down a back, a ring left by a glass of iced tea. Dey contradict each udder on every point dat matters, da t’rower, da range, and da year, and none can be checked against a record. Six of da 8 ain’t never been written down." },
  "The Pump Dynasty": { label: "Da Pump Dynasty", desc: "Of da 4 pump cartel houses, one outlasts da udders: da family come down from da kid who drew da sketch, holdin’ da original page and supplyin’ 7 pumps in 10 across da grid. Every unit it casts comes from dat drawin’, da 1-inch bore and 6-inch stroke scaled by whole numbers and never redesigned, so a 12-times station pump moves about 35 gallons a stroke. Somebody from da family shows up at every first primin’ of da season. Da heirs get raised on graph paper at 4 squares to da inch and talked out of drawin’ anyt’ing else." },
  "The First Pump": { label: "Da First Pump", desc: "An object gets venerated as da original machine: a length of 1-inch PVC wit’ a 6-inch stroke, a dowel for a plunger, and a trimmed faucet washer for a seal, kept in a case at 68°F and brought out once a year. 4 separate polities hold an object described exactly like dat. 3 are plainly later work, 1 of dem threaded for a fittin’ nobody sold till 40 years after da sketch, and da fourth can’t be looked at. Every side figures da question’s settled in its own favor, ya know." },
  "Gauge Chroniclers": { label: "Gauge Chroniclers", desc: "Stuff starts gettin’ written down as it happens instead of remembered afterward. 4 chroniclers work nonstop, loggin’ every reservoir level, opened valve, and confirmed splash, about 900 entries a year across da big basins. Dere accounts don’t agree wit’ da oral canon on roughly a third of what dey both cover, usually by a factor of 10 in gallons and always in da canon’s favor, eh." },
  "Reckoned From the Heat": { label: "Counted From Da Heat", desc: "Time gets counted from da heat itself. Stuff is dated Before Heat and After Heat, countin’ from da first day past 100°F, da year stands at 3 After Heat, and dat’s how it goes in every gauge log kept out dere. A regular year runs 340 days on da local count, from 1 first hot day to da next, wit’ a 12-day gap nobody’s ever squared up." },
  "The Pumping Calendar": { label: "Da Pumpin’ Calendar", desc: "Da year gets divided by da machine: a primin’ season, a pumpin’ season, a season of repackin’ seals, and 11 days set aside for countin’ washers. Dates are given as so many days before or after first stroke, which comes when da 1-inch test barrel at da foundin’ well first draws a full 2.6 ounces. Da civil year and da pumpin’ year drift apart about 1 day a century, and no boss anywhere has ever been willin’ to fix it." },
  "Spigot Rites": { label: "Spigot Rites", desc: "Observances get set up for da ones who fought in da earliest summers, held 4 times a year at 9 old spigots, each one opened for exactly 1 minute in dere honor. Da biggest one draws over 2,000. Da named honorees number 63, includin’ da kid wit’ da window-cleaner spray bottle and da dog whose bowl got t’rown, and about half of ’em only show up in da oral canon." },
  "Barrow Tanks": { label: "Barrow Tanks", desc: "Da barrows of da earliest fighters are da highest ground on da plain, and every 1 foot of height is worth 0.43 psi at da bottom of a pipe. So da mounds get fortified wit’ water: 11 tanks go up on da 11 tallest barrows, each 30 feet across, and da towns below get dere pressure from da dead. Holdin’ a barrow now means holdin’ da water over a whole district. Da dead don’t mind, dey say, since da job’s mostly standin’ still." },
  "Memorial Fountains": { label: "Memorial Fountains", desc: "Dere’s memorials now, 61 of ’em across da basin. Each one’s a fountain recirculatin’ da same 40 gallons, kept up by folks who never knew da honored and visited on a set schedule, 4 observances a year, da biggest drawin’ over 2,000. Toppin’ up what da heat takes is a paid job. Da oldest fountain has run 11 months wit’out once gettin’ shut off." },
  "Buried Pumps": { label: "Buried Pumps", desc: "Pumps get buried wit’ dere crews, handle down and check valve shut, on da understandin’ dat da work keeps goin’. Dug-up units turn up wit’ water still standin’ in da barrel, held dere by a valve nobody ever asked to open again. Da rubber washer picked off a hardware-store peg for 40 cents, chosen ’cause it sealed a 1-inch bore across a 6-inch stroke, holds for 11 centuries underground, which is longer dan any reservoir, any compact, and any story about why da war started." },
  "Interregnum": { label: "Interregnum", desc: "Da central control room goes dark for 109 days, wit’ no recognized dispatcher anywhere on da grid. 19 utilities set dere own pressures, 4 of ’em by droppin’ each udder’s, deliveries fall by 60 percent, and 2 in 3 pump stations sit empty. Da gauge archive gets moved twice in pickup trucks and loses about a tent’ of its binders." },
  "Age of Warring Reservoirs": { label: "Age of Warrin’ Reservoirs", desc: "Centuries of fightin’ get squeezed into a single heat wave. 9 dynasties rise up around 9 reservoirs, push dere canals past da reach of dere own pressure at an average of 2 miles, and fall apart before da evenin’ cooldown, which never really shows up anyhow. Average dynasty lasts 31 hours, and no line holds its dam more dan twice, ya know." },
  "Death Valley Standoff": { label: "Deat’ Valley Standoff", desc: "Da air hits 130°F, near da highest temperature ever reliably measured on Earth, and da standoff is now between people and da afternoon. Each reservoir fort grows a town around and under its walls, ’cause da walls are da closest shade and da water behind ’em keeps da cellars near 85°F. A person wit’out water or shade can lose 5 percent of body weight in sweat in about 3 hours. Inside 2 weeks fort and town are 1 settlement, and nobody goes outside between 11 and 6." },
  "Pump Warlords": { label: "Pump Warlords", desc: "At a wet-bulb of 33°C, about 91°F at full humidity, sweat barely cools nobody, and whoever holds a workin’ pump holds da ground. 9 warlords get counted, each one sized up not by territory but by how many hand pumps dey can keep primed, wit’ plungers whole and check valves dat still close. Alliances get made in units of pumps and paid for in shade. Da biggest holdin’ runs to 4,000 pumps and controls no shade of any kind, so its people drink first and get under cover last." },
  "Last Cool Water": { label: "Last Cool Water", desc: "At a wet-bulb of 34°C, stayin’ alive runs on works built in a calmer century: buried cisterns under domes, fed by underground channels and vented by windcatchers, 9 of da original 11 still in use. Dere water holds near 71°F and gets shared by measure, 250 mL every 15 minutes per person, about 1 liter an hour, kids and old folks first. Later builders copy da proportions, 9 feet of tower over da roof for every 5 feet of cistern below, to wit’in inches, wit’out ever figurin’ out why da old ones run cooler." },
  "The Mythic Pump": { label: "Da Mythic Pump", desc: "Da wet-bulb hits 35°C, about 95°F at full saturation, sweat stops coolin’ anybody, and a healthy person out in da open has roughly 6 hours. In da rotation of who sits nearest da fan, da old folks tell about a pump dat never runs dry, liftin’ cold water for a whole province in one night, worked by a kid. Every piece of it is da original spec, a 1-inch bore and a 6-inch stroke at 2.6 ounces a pull, blown up by a factor of about 10,000. Nothin’ in it is made up except da scale and da cold." },
  "Dhahran Accord": { label: "Dhahran Accord", desc: "Dhahran, Saudi Arabia, July 2003: a dew point of 95°F under 108°F air, and a heat index near 176°F. Da accord drafted at dis tier has 3 clauses, water is shared, shade is shared, and nobody gets left outside, and it’s da last t’ing da water war keeps on purpose. By now da war’s got a calendar, a canon of 400 entries, its own dead, and its own foundin’ myths. No record of da first flicked fingertip survives, and da earliest event anybody can confirm comes about 6 summers after it." },
  "The Great Cannons": { label: "Da Great Cannons", desc: "Past 134°F da war builds water cannons as monuments as much as weapons: 9 of ’em, da biggest wit’ a 40-foot barrel t’rowin’ 1,100 feet. At dis temperature a stream dat long shows up about 20% lighter, and da loss gets logged as a tax paid to da air. Each cannon’s got a name and each has a crew dat does nothin’ else. Later ages rebuild 4 of ’em off da original drawin’s, and none of da 4 t’rows as far." },
  "The Great Pump Engines": { label: "Da Great Pump Engines", desc: "At 140°F a stream loses half its mass crossin’ a street, so water stays inside da pipe, and da pumps grow to da size of da age: barrels 12 feet across wit’ 72-foot strokes, worked t’rough a walkin’ wheel by 40 oxen at an ox’s pace. Da proportions are unchanged from da 1-inch prototype to 3 decimal places, ’cause da drawin’ scaled and nobody saw why ya’d redesign what worked. 9 get built. 4 survive as monuments." },
  "The Written Ledger": { label: "Da Written Ledger", desc: "At 160°F tap water scalds skin in about 1 second, so nobody’s t’rowin’ nothin’ and da scribes take over. Da oral tradition gets put in writin’ for da first time, 1,200 entries across 9 volumes, every splash since da condensation ring. Fights dat ran for summers get settled in an afternoon by checkin’ da page. 4 older accounts get formally retired, and da archive gets copied twice against loss, in ink dat don’t run." },
  "The Stepwell": { label: "Da Stepwell", desc: "At 180°F a squirt partly flashes to vapor wit’in 3 feet of da nozzle, so water gets kept where da air can’t get at it: in a stepwell 9 levels deep, a pyramid built upside down. Each terrace is its own defensive line, and da water at da bottom, 100 feet below da rim, stays near 90°F. Attackers gotta go down 3,500 steps under fire from every landin’. None of ’em has reached da water, which is da whole idea, eh." },
  "The Pump Codex": { label: "Da Pump Codex", desc: "At 212°F open water boils off all by itself, and da scattered pump manuals get collected, squared up, and set down as one work of 1,100 pages. It covers every bore table, valve condition, and seal failure recorded in 400 years, from da 1-inch original on up. Where sources don’t agree, both readin’s get printed. Da rule “A pump moves water; it makes none” shows up on da first page and da last, word for word, and it’s da only passage in da codex wit’ no variant readin’s at all." },
  "The Long Ceasefire": { label: "Da Long Ceasefire", desc: "At 230°F a full bucket boils dry in under an hour, and da fightin’ stops for da longest stretch on record: 41 days wit’out a confirmed splash. Cannons and stepwells get kept up but not manned, stockpiles hold at 9,000 gallons in sealed tanks, and 8 in 10 fighters take up buildin’ or archive work instead. Da kettles get put away wit’ dere whistles taped. No paper anywhere calls it permanent." },
  "Ceasefire Pumping": { label: "Ceasefire Pumpin’", desc: "Under da long peace da pumps get put to civil work: keepin’ da sealed household lines charged at about 20 psi over da air, which is what water needs at 260°F to stay liquid. Crews still set up in wartime divisions work 60,000 pumps every mornin’, 1-inch bores and up. Da park don’t get cut. Both sides know darn well dat a civil pump fleet is a military pump fleet doin’ somet’ing else, and both leave it exactly like it is." },
  "Wars of Reconfluence": { label: "Wars of Reconfluence", desc: "At 300°F da ceasefire ends and da successor basins get shoved back together over a 14-month campaign. 26 settlements get swallowed up, 6 by treaty and 20 by assault, givin’ up 200,000 gallons of sealed stockpile between ’em, and da front stretches past 9 miles. Every shot is pressurized water dat flashes to steam leavin’ da barrel, so peak use of 90,000 gallons a day makes fog banks instead of hits, two thirds of it from massed cannons in ranked lines of 60." },
  "Pressure Trains": { label: "Pressure Trains", desc: "At 330°F a cannon’s water has to ride at about 90 psi just to stay liquid, so artillery moves as its own outfit: 60 cannons wit’ dere crews, spare seals, and pressure tanks, coverin’ 9 miles a day on packed roads. Da train shows up 4 days behind da advance and decides when an assault can start. 2 trains get lost in da campaign, both to tanks bustin’ in da heat instead of to any fightin’." },
  "Pump Trains": { label: "Pump Trains", desc: "Pumps get mounted in strings on da siege railways, 40 to a train, keepin’ da pressure lines along a movin’ front charged at about 180 psi, which is what water needs at 380°F to stay water. One train recharges 11 miles of line in a night, every plunger aboard strokin’ in time. Da trains carry no weapons, never get shot at by either side, and are da only rigs in da war allowed to cross da line, under a standin’ deal nobody ever wrote down." },
  "Successor Basins": { label: "Successor Basins", desc: "At 430°F da reunited territory busts up again inside a season into 5 polities holdin’ between 2 and 11 settlements each. Borders get drawn on da master map to da foot and redrawn on average every 19 days, on paper dat’ll catch fire by itself at about 451°F, which da mapmakers bring up at every meetin’. Combined standin’ forces top 40,000, and none of ’em holds a third." },
  "Colonial Pumps": { label: "Colonial Pumps", desc: "Da pump goes overseas ahead of da armies, ’cause ground wit’ a primed well can be held. 400 units get landed da first season wit’ crews, castin’ patterns, and a copy of da codex etched on steel plate, since paper catches fire all by itself now at 490°F. Territories get surveyed and claimed in pumpin’ radii, da 3 miles a station can hold a line at 600 psi, and 3 modern boundaries still follow da arcs." },
  "The Outpost Era": { label: "Da Outpost Era", desc: "At 550°F expeditions push out past da war’s original basins, foundin’ 31 outposts at ranges of 6 to 40 miles, each one claimin’ da water wit’in 1 mile of its charter well. Each carries a steel charter, 8,000 gallons in brazed tanks, since soldered joints let go way back near 450°F, 1 cannon in pieces, and a return schedule it don’t keep. 11 outposts lose contact inside da first 2 months." },
  "Canister Batteries": { label: "Canister Batteries", desc: "At 620°F water only travels in sealed steel canisters holdin’ about 1,800 psi, which makes it, finally, a real artillery round. Canisters get fired 2 to 4 miles past da horizon off map coordinates alone, wit’ flight times of 90 seconds, so targets get led instead of aimed at. 1 round in 40 lands in da settlement it was meant for, and volume makes up da difference. Da lead weights on da range tables are 1 degree from meltin’." },
  "The Supercritical Grid": { label: "Da Supercritical Grid", desc: "At 705°F and 3,200 psi water passes its critical point and ain’t liquid or gas, and pumps are da only t’ing left dat can tell it where to go. Distribution gets planned as one grid across da hegemony: numbered lines, scheduled strokes, and a standin’ pressure held over 400,000 square miles by pumps come down from da 1-inch original. Weather turns into an input to da schedule instead of a source of water. In an average year da grid moves more water dan’s fallen as rain in 40 years, and dat gets reported like any udder statistic." },
  "Supercritical Hegemony": { label: "Supercritical Hegemony", desc: "At 780°F one polity piles up enough of an edge to set terms for all da udders: 62 percent of standin’ forces and 70 percent of da supercritical reserve. Da udder 4 states keep dere flags, dere calendars, and none of dere foreign policy. Da deal holds 200 days, endin’ right after da air passes 787°F and da zinc runs off every galvanized tank da hegemony owns." },
  "The Reservoir Arcology": { label: "Da Reservoir Arcology", desc: "At 870°F da outdoors matches da surface of Venus, and da city moves inside its reservoir: 1 sealed structure 2 miles across, holdin’ 3 million people around a core of water at 3,200 psi. Da walls are 40 feet of ceramic and steel, and da inside gets held at 75°F by a refrigeration plant da size of a hill. Nobody inside has been outdoors in 6 years, and da only weather report is a pressure gauge in da lobby." },
  "The Pump Fleet": { label: "Da Pump Fleet", desc: "Redistribution gets projected: 1,100 crawler tankers, each one carryin’ 12 station pumps of da 1-inch line, crews, castin’ patterns, and sealed water, sent across da dry seabeds to any coast da hegemony wants to see wet. At 970°F da old oceans are a salt flat, and a fleet showin’ up is taken as a statement about who owns da ground under it. No landin’ in 200 years gets opposed, ’cause opposin’ it would take a fleet, and dere’s 1 fleet." },
  "The Cistern Archive": { label: "Da Cistern Archive", desc: "A vault 400 feet down holds da whole record: 9,000 volumes on steel sheet, 300 maps, and da master gauge census. Wit’ da surface at 1,100°F, da vault holds at 72°F all year inside a water jacket dat never gets drained, and it never moves more dan 1 degree. Gettin’ in takes 2 keys held by different offices, and both get used every week." },
  "The Pattern Vault": { label: "Da Pattern Vault", desc: "Every bore table, valve drawin’, and seal spec ever used gets stored in 1 sealed vault, engraved on nickel plate in a form meant to be readable wit’out any pump left to check it against. 11,000 patterns get put in. Da earliest is da graph-paper sketch at 4 squares to da inch, still creased in 8 and dated in crayon, and it goes in unflattened on strict orders from da archivists. Da vault gets sealed at 1,200°F, 21 degrees short of where every aluminum pump on da surface melts." },
  "Reservoir Siege": { label: "Reservoir Siege", desc: "At 1,300°F a single reservoir fortress gets surrounded and holds. Da siege runs 840 days and burns t’rough an estimated 4 million gallons fired in sealed canisters by 9 standin’ cannons never once taken apart, and it outlasts da government dat ordered it. Da garrison rotates 11 times and da besiegers 9, and neither original commander sees da end of it. Da fortress windows sag 2 inches over da whole t’ing." },
  "Perpetual Cannons": { label: "Perpetual Cannons", desc: "At 1,500°F da cannons dat never quit firin’ pass out of livin’ memory and into inheritance: 11 of ’em, da oldest in steady service for 240 years, every pressure seal swapped on schedule while da barrel’s still hot. Crews get born into dere spots and trained from age 6. Da maintenance logs run longer dan da stories of da campaigns da cannons got built for." },
  "Cathedral Pumps": { label: "Cathedral Pumps", desc: "Pumps get built right into da halls dey keep cool, 1-inch plungers by da t’ousand runnin’ inside da walls and pushin’ water t’rough roof and buttress on a set liturgical cycle. Nobody works da handles; da strokes get driven off da same 1,600°F heat dat would udderwise crack da masonry. Da circulation gets renewed every day for 900 years wit’out an operator ever goin’ inside da mechanism." },
  "Water Cathedrals": { label: "Water Cathedrals", desc: "At 1,800°F buildin’ turns to structures wit’ no military use at all: 9 halls, da tallest 90 feet to da vault, each one built around a pool of liquid water held at 70°F, da only open water in 1,000 miles. Each takes 4 to 6 years. Walls run 12 feet t’ick at da base, firebrick faced wit’ 1 inch of ceramic glaze redone every season, and combined labor tops 200,000 person-hours." },
  "The Pump Houses": { label: "Da Pump Houses", desc: "At 2,000°F, wit’ da ground startin’ to run as lava, comin’ down from a pump crew turns into a claim to authority, and 4 houses trace demselves back to named crews of da classical period. 2 of da 4 lines are documented and 2 are just claimed. Who goes first at court gets decided by da number of 2.6-ounce strokes an ancestor is recorded as pullin’, a figure out of a registry started back when dere were 406 pumps in da whole world." },
  "The Hollow Aquifer": { label: "Da Hollow Aquifer", desc: "At 2,300°F da surface rock is melted down to 30 feet, and da strongest fort on da planet is a mountain hollowed out around its own aquifer. Galleries run 9 miles into granite dat melts near 2,200°F at da surface and is still only 140°F 2 miles in. Da water table sits 4,000 feet below da summit. Da mountain’s got no gates, ’cause da mountain is da gate, eh." },
  "Aquifer Dynasties": { label: "Aquifer Dynasties", desc: "At 2,500°F rule passes down documented lines of 6 to 9 generations, at an average reign of 14 years, each line holdin’ 1 aquifer. Succession gets fought out in court instead of in da field and settled by da gauge archive in 8 cases outta 10. Da longest unbroken line has held its capital 110 years, and its water table dropped 3 feet in dat time, which da dynasty calls stable." },
  "Subsurface Pumps": { label: "Subsurface Pumps", desc: "At 2,800°F iron melts at da surface, and every pump still workin’ is underground, where da rock’s cooler. In galleries no surface crew will ever see, 900 pumps lift water outta fossil aquifers 10,000 years old, so loaded wit’ minerals it scales a barrel shut in a week. Plungers get run at a third of da regular rate ’cause anyt’ing faster cooks da seals. None of ’em ever comes back up, since da trip would melt it." },
  "Shaft Cannons": { label: "Shaft Cannons", desc: "At 3,100°F da surface is a sea of melted rock, and cannons get set in da cool rock under it, firin’ canisters up t’rough bored shafts: 60 of ’em at depths near 900 feet. Ya can’t see ’em, can’t shoot back at ’em, and can’t move ’em. Every shaft gets re-bored each season as da melt eats down into it about 20 feet a year." },
  "The Cool Interior": { label: "Da Cool Interior", desc: "At 3,500°F da crust is deep enough to hold a livable interior: 1,100 miles of mapped passage, 400 chambers, and a permanent population of 90,000, all wit’in reach of da deep water. Surface travel goes from rare to flat-out impossible. Inside temperature holds at 86°F every season, warm by da old standards and da coolest spot on da planet." },
  "Pumps Beneath the Crust": { label: "Pumps Under Da Crust", desc: "At 3,800°F whole pump works run under 2 miles of rock, liftin’, pipin’, and recirculatin’ water t’rough chambers dat only exist ’cause a pump drained ’em. Crews get born, trained, and buried wit’out ever comin’ up. Dere bore tables are unchanged from da codex, 1 inch and 6 inches scaled by whole numbers, but da way dey say da primin’ count has drifted so far from da surface way dat da 2 can’t understand each udder no more." },
  "The Deep Nations": { label: "Da Deep Nations", desc: "At 4,300°F separate peoples show up below da melt, 7 of ’em, split by rock instead of distance, in spots by as little as 40 feet of it. Dialects drift past understandin’ each udder inside 3 generations, t’ough all 7 keep da same word for water. Gettin’ between da 2 farthest apart takes a 9-day trip t’rough neutral passage, carryin’ its own water." },
  "The Pump Nations": { label: "Da Pump Nations", desc: "At 4,800°F political identity settles on da pump instead of on territory: peoples get counted by which bore pattern dey hold and which stroke table dey follow. 4 nations, 11 patterns, and no borders ya could draw on a surface map, ’cause dere’s no surface left to draw one on. War between ’em gets fought almost entirely by keepin’ plunger seals away from each udder." },
  "The Buried Summers": { label: "Da Buried Summers", desc: "At 5,300°F everyt’ing from da cool centuries lies under hardened melt: every original yard, every first spigot, every kiddie pool keep, at depths of 160 feet. Diggin’ turns into a recognized trade employin’ 4,000. 11 original sites get recovered, 1 of ’em holdin’ 3,000 balloons long since fused into a single carbon slab shaped like a laundry basket, and 9 get reburied on purpose." },
  "The Buried Pump Works": { label: "Da Buried Pump Works", desc: "At 5,900°F a whole pump works gets dug out from under 900 feet of rock, castin’ patterns whole, wit’ a run of 1-inch barrels still in da racks. Dey turn out identical to current production wit’in a tent’ of a millimeter, 6-inch strokes and all. Da find ends a 4-century scholarly argument about whether da classical proportions were ever really used, and lets down both sides of it." },
  "The Well Keep": { label: "Da Well Keep", desc: "At 6,500°F even tungsten melts, and da last fort is a well: a shaft 1 kilometer deep wit’ 60 floors stacked down its walls and da water table at da bottom. Each floor gets held by da folks who live on it, and an attacker’s gotta go down t’rough every one of ’em to get to da water. Da bucket rope is 3,281 feet long and is da most guarded t’ing in da keep." },
  "The Undercrust Polity": { label: "Da Undercrust Polity", desc: "At 7,300°F one administration covers da whole livable underground: 200,000 square miles of passage, a census of 1.4 million, and a standin’ force of 200,000. Orders take 19 days to get to da far frontier by tunnel. Water gets measured in tons a day instead of gallons an hour, against a standin’ park of 40,000 pumps." },
  "Margin Pumping": { label: "Margin Pumpin’", desc: "At 8,100°F, past da meltin’ point of every known material, pumpin’ runs at da size of da whole interior: 11,000 units workin’ da edges where cool rock meets da melt, pushin’ water back into ground da heat dried out. Dey run a steady circuit of 4,000 miles, 1-inch line down to da last plunger. Da circuit takes a lifetime to get around once, and crews inherit dere spot on it." },
  "Envelope Weather": { label: "Envelope Weather", desc: "At 9,000°F, close to da temperature of da Sun’s surface, water vapor stops stayin’ water and comes apart into hydrogen, oxygen, and hydroxyl faster dan it can re-form. Da boiled-off oceans hang over da planet as an envelope pressin’ down at about 270 atmospheres, and da envelope makes its own weather: 41 storms logged in a single year, none of dem wet. Cloud tops climb 230 miles. Yah, da heat wave dat started da war is now a minor entry in a record it no longer drives." },
  "Envelope Pumps": { label: "Envelope Pumps", desc: "At 10,000°F, da temperature of da Sun’s surface, da pump is rebuilt to work in gas dat glows: da tube becomes a magnetic field, da plunger a ring of current, and bore and stroke keep da original 1-to-6 proportion, scaled up 40,000 times, eh. It still makes nothin’; it moves da 2-to-1 mix of hydrogen and oxygen dat used to be da oceans, and where it pushes decides which hemisphere is merely 10,000°F. Pumpin’ and runnin’ da climate are now da same job. Da office dat sets da stroke schedule descends, by unbroken succession, from da council dat wrote Section 14-212 on emergency hand pumps." },
  "Planetary Water Order": { label: "Planetary Water Order", desc: "At 14,000°F hydrogen begins sheddin’ its electrons and da ground glows white. Holy wah, governance unifies across every remainin’ holdin’ under a single charter of 900 articles, 211 of dem on who may keep ice. Population stands at 90 million, 96 percent of it in refrigerated deep shafts and on outer moons, wit’ 4 million under arms. Da last independent cistern accedes after a 7-year negotiation over 11 gallons." },
  "Equatorial Moat": { label: "Equatorial Moat", desc: "At 20,000°F da ground runs hotter dan da surface of Sirius, about 17,400°F, and da planet gets a fort to match: a moat around da equator, 24,901 miles long and 40 miles wide. It holds no water in da usual sense, only a 2-to-1 plasma of hydrogen and oxygen kept in its channel by field coils every 900 feet. Both hemispheres claim it as dere outer defense. It is da first water feature in da war visible from another planet, and nobody can swim in it, ya know." },
  "Planetary Pump Order": { label: "Planetary Pump Order", desc: "At 29,000°F, about da surface temperature of a hot blue-white star, one authority holds every pattern, every check valve, and every crew on da planet, and issues a single stroke schedule for every pump. Compliance is total ’cause non-compliance means no spare washers, eh. Da schedule is published 100 years in advance and has been revised 11 times in 4,000 years, each revision by less dan a day. Every unit on it still pulls a stroke 6 times da width of its bore." },
  "The Long Dry Compact": { label: "Da Long Dry Compact", desc: "At 41,000°F, about da surface temperature of da star Spica, an agreement is signed to end da war permanently. It runs to 11,000 articles, 900 of dem on decommissionin’ pumps, and is ratified by every polity still holdin’ water. It holds 31 years, da longest peace on record, before da first violation: 4 participants and 9 squirts of 2.6 ounces each. Ya know, hostilities resume within a month." },
  "Free-Fall Pump": { label: "Free-Fall Pump", desc: "At 59,000°F, hotter dan a lightnin’ bolt, da war needs pumps for a place wit’ no ground to stand on, which breaks da one assumption da design has carried since da PVC prototype: dat da plunger moves ’cause somebody braced dere feet and pulled. In free fall, pullin’ da handle mostly pulls da puller, and da water does not fall toward da intake. 9 designs are proposed. Yah, da one adopted keeps da handle, braced against a track, purely so da plunger still stops when da pullin’ stops." },
  "Orbital Pumps": { label: "Orbital Pumps", desc: "At 84,000°F, about da surface temperature of da hottest O-type stars, pumpin’ leaves da ground, eh. Platforms in orbit carry barrels wit’ a 400-foot bore and a 2,400-foot stroke, da original 1-to-6 proportion scaled up 4,800 times, so each pull moves about 2.25 billion gallons of comet water. Plungers cycle once a minute on a fixed timetable. Time from a soakin’ order to water on da target is 19 minutes, and da operators are never seen." },
  "Drop Pumping": { label: "Drop Pumping", desc: "At 120,000°F, about da surface temperature of a young white dwarf, water is delivered from orbit by release instead of by nozzle: each stroke drops a metered slug of ice timed to shatter and spread on da way down. Holy wah, scatter runs to 40 percent of every slug, against about 3 percent da PVC prototype lost t’rough its leakin’ elbow. Da technique is used for 100 years, is never made to work properly, and is abandoned for pumps dat stay in orbit and push slowly." },
  "Suborbital Lobs": { label: "Suborbital Lobs", desc: "At 170,000°F a hot surface glows mostly in extreme ultraviolet, and rounds leave da air entirely between launch and landin’. Insulated ice slugs of 40 gallons are lobbed 900 miles on 11-minute flights. Launch sites are fixed, hardened, and known to both sides, which makes destroyin’ dem da only worthwhile target. 400 are in service and none has been moved in a decade, ya know." },
  "Ring Pumps": { label: "Ring Pumps", desc: "At 240,000°F da orbital pumps are coupled end to end into continuous rings, barrels joined around da whole planet and fed from Saturn ring ice, which is about 99 percent water. 3 rings at 3 inclinations spray a band dat closes on itself and wets da entire surface on a 19-day cycle, eh. From da ground da rings show at dusk as a faint line, and in 4 languages da word for dat line is da word for hose." },
  "The Orbital Ice Dome": { label: "Da Orbital Ice Dome", desc: "At 350,000°F, approachin’ da surface temperature of da hottest known white dwarfs, a dome goes up in orbit by exactly da method of da backyard one: blocks laid in a single risin’ spiral, each leanin’ on da last, no scaffoldin’. Da blocks are cut from ring ice 9 feet on a side, and da spiral runs 140 turns before it closes at da crown. Inside are 9 billion gallons and a garrison of 200. Ya know, nobody can explain why da spiral runs counterclockwise, only dat it always has." },
  "Half-Planet Waterworks": { label: "Half-Planet Waterworks", desc: "At 490,000°F oxygen holds on to just 3 of its 8 electrons. One hemisphere falls entirely under a single water authority; da udder organizes against it within 2 years. Da front runs da full equator, 24,901 miles, held at an average of 900 personnel and 4 pump batteries per mile. Yah, neither side has advanced a net mile in a decade." },
  "No Dry Rear": { label: "No Dry Rear", desc: "At 700,000°F, da temperature of da thin layer between da Sun’s surface and its corona, da war reaches every point of every holdin’ at once and dere is no dry rear anywhere, eh. 400 million are enrolled on one side or da udder, and da neutral population stands at 0. Daily expenditure passes 9 billion rounds, nearly all of it t’rough gas-pressure batteries runnin’ without pause, against a supply of roughly 1 trillion comets dat no longer counts as finite." },
  "Sky Pumps": { label: "Sky Pumps", desc: "At 1,000,000°F, roughly a third as hot as da Sun’s corona, da pumps stop sprayin’ targets and start pumpin’ into da sky, meterin’ vapor into da circulation of da war’s cooler worlds so it arrives as weather. Holy wah, delivery from order to rainfall runs 11 days, and da target area is a continent. Each plunger still strokes 6 times its bore. It is da first pumpin’ in da war’s history dat produces somet’in’ indistinguishable, by any test either side can devise, from real rain." },
  "Pressure Batteries": { label: "Pressure Batteries", desc: "At 5.4 million °F, hotter dan most of da Sun’s corona, squirt mechanisms are retired for gas pressure: batteries driven off vessels charged by da weight of da comet ice dey sit on, t’rowin’ slugs at 1,100 feet per second. One battery matches da daily output of 900 three-person balloon launchers. Da last balloon launcher is decommissioned da same year, its surgical tubin’ coiled, tagged, and moved to da archive, ya know." },
  "The Master Cam": { label: "Da Master Cam", desc: "At 29 million °F, hotter dan da core of da Sun, every pump in da system is slaved to a single master cam: a steel lobe 9 miles around whose profile sets each plunger’s stroke without reference to any position on da ground. It is reground continuously by a crew of 400 who never see a pump, eh. Frontage under active pumpin’ is reported in millions of square miles. Da last human review of da cam profile is dated 400 years before da current run began, and none has been needed since." },
  "Planetary Reflooding": { label: "Planetary Reflooding", desc: "At 160 million °F, just short of da roughly 180 million °F at which old stars begin fusin’ helium into carbon, da pump reaches da limit of its purpose: a whole planet’s water is drawn from da poles and spread evenly to da equator by pumps too tall to see da top of. Da proportions survive intact from da graph paper, a bore of 1 part to a stroke of 6, wit’ 1 check valve per barrel. Ya know, what began as a way to soak 1 sibling is da only t’ing standin’ between a world and a dry one." },
  "Pumps Out of Contact": { label: "Pumps Outta Contact", desc: "At 860 million °F, short of da 1.1 billion °F at which massive stars start burnin’ carbon, pumps dispatched beyond da range of any order keep strokin’ to dere last received schedule, on worlds nobody has surveyed, for reasons nobody has restated. 11,000 units are carried on da books as operational and unreachable. Yah, dere delivery is presumed exact, ’cause a check valve left alone has never once let water run backward." },
  "Ocean World Conflict": { label: "Ocean World Conflict", desc: "At 4.6 billion °F, hot enough to fuse oxygen into silicon in da core of a dyin’ star, da war’s main moon is entirely flooded: an ocean wit’ no shore, averagin’ 580 feet deep, and da war is da sole organizin’ activity of da species, eh. Open water absorbs about 94 percent of da light dat reaches it, so da ocean never freezes and nobody ever runs out. Da engagement has run 900 years wit’ no recorded month of ceasefire and no hour in which some pump is not firin’." },
  "Crater Lake Fort": { label: "Crater Lake Fort", desc: "At 25 billion °F, da temperature of da universe about 1 second old, light turns freely into electron and positron pairs and neutrinos stop noticin’ anyt’ing. Holy wah, a lunar crater serves as a ready-made round fort: Shackleton, at da Moon’s south pole, 13 miles across and 2.6 miles deep, its floor in permanent shadow and thought to hold ice. No corners, an equal arc of fire, and da moat already frozen in. It is da backyard round fort, only very much bigger." },
  "Lunar Pump Yards": { label: "Lunar Pump Yards", desc: "At 140 billion °F protons and neutrons turn into one another freely, in equal numbers. Manufacture moves off-world entirely, castin’ barrels in low gravity, where a 400-foot plunger tube can be poured in 1 piece without a joint. Da original was cut from PVC wit’ 1 hacksaw and joined at an elbow, and every barrel since has kept da joint. Da elbow disappears from da design after some 30,000 years. Nothin’ else about da drawin’ changes, ya know." },
  "Off-World Campaign": { label: "Off-World Campaign", desc: "At 730 billion °F, about a quarter of da way to meltin’ protons, fightin’ extends off da surface entirely: 11 orbital positions, 4 of dem permanently garrisoned, wit’ transit times measured in days. Ice slugs are launched from surface mass drivers at 9,000 feet per second and take 4 hours to arrive, eh. Da oldest held position has been under spray for 60 years." },
  "Comet Pumps": { label: "Comet Pumps", desc: "At 4 trillion °F protons and neutrons melt into a soup of quarks and gluons, da state made for an instant in lead-ion collisions at da Large Hadron Collider. Pumps are bolted onto comets and set to pump dem outward, so da supply and da pump are da same object. A comet 1 mile across is about 80 percent ice, which gives each unit 9,000 years of strokes. 40 are dispatched. Ya know, none is expected to report and none is designed to stop." },
  "Comet Cistern": { label: "Comet Cistern", desc: "At 22 trillion °F da heat beats da roughly 10 trillion °F of da hottest collisions ever made at da Large Hadron Collider. A comet is heaped and hollowed by da method used on da backyard heap: pile da rubble, let it set, dig it out. Da rubble is 3 miles of loose ice, left 40 years to sinter, den dug out by 600 crews into a cistern wit’ walls 900 feet thick. Yah, it holds water on da inside instead of t’rowin’ it, which is considered a novelty." },
  "Projected Fire": { label: "Projected Fire", desc: "At 120 trillion °F even da heavy charm and bottom quarks are made and unmade freely in da soup, eh. Engagements now run at distances where da target has moved before da round arrives, and both sides fire at where da udder is calculated to be. Transit runs 4 months, and confirmation of a hit takes 4 more. Da ledger is kept on projections, and da projections are always soaked." },
  "Pumps at Range": { label: "Pumps at Range", desc: "At 630 trillion °F da universe is less dan a billionth of a second old. Holy wah, water is delivered across distances at which da pump and its target are never in contact, accelerated and released to arrive as an even spray after a transit measured in years, wit’ aim corrected for da target world’s motion over da whole flight. Delivery on arrival is, by da survivin’ figures, da same 2.6 ounces per scaled stroke a kid once measured into a cup on a kitchen counter." },
  "The Oort Front": { label: "Da Oort Front", desc: "At 3.4 quadrillion °F da electromagnetic and weak forces stop bein’ separate t’ings, da last well-mapped landmark before da physics turns speculative. Da war’s farthest boundary sits in da Oort cloud, 900 million miles past da last charted supply post, held by 2 formations unchanged in 4,000 years. Ground changes hands at 11 inches a century in either direction. Supply, succession, and doctrine remain open questions, argued before da archive at 96 cases a year, ya know." },
  "Girdle Pumps": { label: "Girdle Pumps", desc: "At 18 quadrillion °F every known particle is massless, da Higgs field havin’ not yet switched on. Pumps are no longer placed on a world but built around one: a ring-shaped barrel 11,000 miles around wit’ a single plunger circlin’ inside it once every 4 days, sprayin’ a band across da whole surface as da world turns beneath it, eh. 4 are known to exist. None has ever been switched off, ’cause no drawin’ in da archive, at any scale, includes a way to stop da plunger udder dan lettin’ go of da handle." },
  "Self-Replicating Pumps": { label: "Self-Replicating Pumps", desc: "At 100 quadrillion °F each particle carries about 5 trillion electron-volts, da scale of a single collision at da Large Hadron Collider, everywhere at once. Da pumps build pumps: loaded wit’ raw polymer instead of water, one works lays out da parts for a second, which lays out a third, and da count doubles every 11 days wit’ no further order. No operator authorized da run and none can find da unit dat would stop it. Ya know, da plungers stroke, da check valves seat, and da water goes out evenly, whether or not anyone still wants a water fight." },
  "Automatic Fire": { label: "Automatic Fire", desc: "At 540 quadrillion °F da heat is beyond anyt’ing any machine on any world has ever produced. Command lapses entirely and nothin’ about da fightin’ changes. Batteries load, fire, and repair themselves on standin’ orders issued 4,000 years before da last officer able to read dem died, and 9 relay stations pass firin’ solutions between positions wit’ no livin’ crew. Yah, da latest inspection found 96 percent of emplacements still soakin’ to original specification." },
  "The Pump Mind": { label: "Da Pump Mind", desc: "At 2.9 quintillion °F da universe is about 10^-17 seconds old, eh. Da pump fleets coordinate without instruction, settlin’ delivery conflicts between units light-hours apart by each holdin’ to its schedule and yieldin’ to da older order. Dere is no mind, and da behavior is indistinguishable from one. It is studied for 900 years by parties who conclude dat a very large number of check valves, each doin’ da only t’ing a check valve does, is what dey have been lookin’ at da whole time." },
  "The Inherited Grudge": { label: "Da Inherited Grudge", desc: "At 16 quintillion °F no experiment has ever reached even a fiftieth of da heat, and da physics is extrapolation. Holy wah, nobody on da line can say what da war is about. Da cause survives as one citation, volume 9, article 412, and da volume it cites was lost about 6,000 years ago. 4 reconstructions have been attempted: 2 say it began wit’ a glass of water tipped over a head at supper, 2 say a flicked fingertip, and no two agree on who was wronged." },
  "Inherited Pumps": { label: "Inherited Pumps", desc: "At 86 quintillion °F da universe is about 10^-20 seconds old. Whole pump formations pass to successors who did not commission dem, cannot reach dem, and in several cases dispute dat dey exist. Ownership rests on a registry entry copied continuously since dere were 11 pumps on one cul-de-sac. Da entries are almost certainly accurate, and dere is no longer any way to check a single one of dem, ya know." },
  "Maser Batteries": { label: "Maser Batteries", desc: "At 4.6×10^20°F fire is drawn directly off a star, t’rough da clouds of water vapor around it dat amplify microwaves at 22 gigahertz. 9 collectors, each 40 miles across, charge da launchers and t’row ice at 1 percent of light speed, beatin’ every balloon launcher ever built by a factor in da billions, eh. A battery charges in 11 days, and da star is projected to hold dat schedule for another 4 billion years." },
  "Star-Driven Pumps": { label: "Star-Driven Pumps", desc: "At 2.5×10^21°F da handle is replaced by a collector dat strokes da plunger for as long as da starlight holds, which keeps da original rule in da only form still available: da plunger moves while somet’in’ pulls and stops when da pullin’ stops. Output is measured in cubic miles a second. Ya know, da check valve is kept at scale, and its crackin’ pressure is still written as a multiple of a figure measured wit’ a bathroom scale and a 1-inch washer." },
  "A Star Moat, Literally": { label: "A Star Moat, Literally", desc: "At 1.4×10^22°F a moat goes around an actual star, laid out in da bastioned trace of da backyard star fort, every face covered from 2 udders. It runs at 2.7 times Earth’s distance from da Sun, just outside da frost line, da nearest a star like dat allows water to stay ice. Da ring holds about 3 oceans’ worth. Yah, from da outside, it looks like a very large sprinkler pattern seen from above." },
  "Light-Delay Orders": { label: "Light-Delay Orders", desc: "At 7.3×10^22°F orders take longer to arrive dan da situations dat prompted dem last, eh. A directive leavin’ da center reaches da nearest garrison in 9 years and da farthest in 400, by which time da position has changed hands twice or dried up. 11 pump stations are recorded as still floodin’ positions under instructions countermanded 300 years before dey were read." },
  "Pumps Beyond Command": { label: "Pumps Beyond Command", desc: "At 4×10^23°F da delay between an order and its execution outlasts da authority dat issued it. Holy wah, pumps stroke to schedules whose signin’ office fell 4,000 years before da first pull, each barrel still bored at 1 part to a stroke of 6. No order has been countermanded anywhere in da system in livin’ memory, and da procedure for countermandin’ one is a matter of scholarly reconstruction, argued from a single survivin’ diagram of a hand lettin’ go." },
  "The Unseen Opponent": { label: "Da Unseen Opponent", desc: "At 2.1×10^24°F no formation on either side has seen an opposin’ formation in 1,100 years. Spray is maintained against coordinates rather dan anyt’ing observed at dem, and 61 standin’ positions report against an adversary whose present existence cannot be established from any record. 2 commissions examine da question and both conclude dat da sprayin’ should continue regardless, ya know." },
  "Unverified Pumps": { label: "Unverified Pumps", desc: "At 1.2×10^25°F, one candidate era for matter gainin’ its slight edge over antimatter, about 1 extra particle per billion, water turns up on worlds no formation admits to havin’ scheduled, delivered in strokes dat match da drawin’ exactly. Both sides check dere registries and find nothin’, eh. Each concludes da udder is lyin’, which is da more comfortable of da 2 available explanations, da udder bein’ dat a run dispatched before either side existed is still workin’ t’rough its list." },
  "The Watered Systems": { label: "Da Watered Systems", desc: "At 6.3×10^25°F every body within reach carries water: 4 planets, 91 moons, and an uncounted field of lesser rock, at depths from 9 feet to 2 miles. Ammunition is no longer drawn where it is t’rown but shipped from wherever da ice runs thickest, on schedules laid 6 years out. Ya know, da planet da war began on is now da 6th largest source of water in it." },
  "Pump Wrecks": { label: "Pump Wrecks", desc: "At 3.4×10^26°F pumps are found derelict at scales no survivin’ process could build: a barrel 400 miles long, a plunger seized at mid-stroke for 2 million years, a handle stopped at da top of its pull. Da proportions are da classical ones, bore 1 part to stroke 6. Yah, da outlet still carries an arrow and da words “water comes out here.” Nothin’ in da design has changed enough, anywhere in it, to date a single piece." },
  "The Inheritors": { label: "Da Inheritors", desc: "At 1.8×10^27°F da people holdin’ da line are no longer descended from anyone who started it, eh. Three peoples hold da positions, none continuous wit’ da founders and 2 arrived from outside da original system, and all 3 cite da same charter, which none can read in da original. Each claim to da ground rests on 90 years of occupation and on nothin’ older." },
  "Inheritor Pump Crews": { label: "Inheritor Pump Crews", desc: "At 10^28°F dere are populations whose whole function is to walk beside pumps dey did not build, clearin’ intakes dey do not understand, in a discipline passed down by imitation for longer dan any language in it has survived. Holy wah, every mornin’ dey blow t’rough each check valve once from each end, and it passes air 1 way only. Da words of da check mean nothin’ to dem, and it is performed correctly, to da second, every time." },
  "Black Hole Slingshots": { label: "Black Hole Slingshots", desc: "At 5.4×10^28°F rounds are swung around collapsed mass and released on da far side at 96 percent of light speed. Each launcher is a fixed installation 11,000 miles across whose workin’ part is a black hole 6 inches wide, da size of a water balloon and about 8.6 times da mass of Earth. 4 are in service, none moved since emplacement. A round fired today lands in a century whose commanders have not been born, ya know." },
  "Horizon Pumps": { label: "Horizon Pumps", desc: "At 2.9×10^29°F, around da scale where theory expects da strong, weak, and electromagnetic forces to act as one, water is pumped onto surfaces from which no report can return. Orders are issued, executed, and closed on da assumption dat da pump did what it has always done, eh. 9 formations keep a standin’ entry for deliveries dat are, by da physics of where dey were sent, permanently unverifiable and almost certainly exactly 2.6 ounces a scaled stroke." },
  "The Last Bucket": { label: "Da Last Bucket", desc: "At 1.6×10^30°F da universe is about 10^-40 seconds old, and at da end of everyt’ing dere is one piece of cover: an overturned bucket, wit’ someone crouched behind it. It is da oldest kind of cover in dis war, a 5-gallon bucket on its side in a backyard, only dis bucket is 900 miles across and da someone is a formation of 40,000. It stops nothin’ larger dan a squirt. Ya know, nobody has ever proposed leavin’ it." },
  "The Axis Reservoir": { label: "Da Axis Reservoir", desc: "At 8.5×10^30°F one structure sits at da origin of every coordinate either side uses: a reservoir keep of ice 4,100 miles long, 2,600 wide, and 900 high, its walls 500 miles thick at da base and 200 at da crown, da classical 9 to 5 to 2. It has been under construction 11,000 years and never declared finished, and its lowest courses are da only water in da war never fired. It holds da archive, da census, and da standin’ definition of da gallon. Yah, neither side has fired on it, ’cause every position is measured from it." },
  "The Axis Pump": { label: "Da Axis Pump", desc: "At 4.6×10^31°F one pump serves da Axis Reservoir and nothin’ else: a plunger 1,100 miles long runnin’ da length of da northern face, sprayin’ a fresh coat of ice over 4,000 miles of wall on a 19-year cycle, eh. Neither side has ever fired on it, for da same reason neither has fired on da reservoir. It has been resurfacin’ da same structure for 11,000 years and has never once reached da crown before da base needed doin’ again." },
  "One Moving Part": { label: "One Moving Part", desc: "At about 2.5×10^32°F, da Planck temperature, da design reaches its final form and turns out to be da drawin’ from da first afternoon: a tube, a plunger, a handle, and an arrow labeled “water comes out here.” Da plunger is 411,000 miles long in a barrel strung between 2 worlds, bore to stroke still 1 to 6, wit’ 1 check valve. Holy wah, it moves only while somet’in’ pulls da handle and stops da instant da pullin’ stops, so da largest machine either side ever built can be halted by any single hand lettin’ go. Nothin’ in da record shows dat ever havin’ been tried." },
  "Before Water": { label: "Before Water", desc: "At about 2.6×10^32°F dere is nothin’ hotter and nothin’ earlier: no hydrogen, no oxygen, no graph paper, no weather. Da line between wet and dry is no longer contested; it is surveyed, it sits at 0 everywhere, and nothin’ has crossed it in either direction. Every war ever counted fits inside a 1-inch bore wit’ 0 participants, kept da way a shoreline is kept: charted, and not expected to move. Den, for da first time, da temperature starts to fall, ya know." },
  "The Next Rain": { label: "Da Next Rain", desc: "Da universe cools below about 3,600°F, and water can hold togedder for da first time since da last war ended. Da first molecules form around 200 million years in, in da leftovers of stars dat never find out what dey were for. 9.1 billion years later, on an unnamed rock 7,900 miles across, it rains: about two miles of it, over roughly a thousand years, which is da longest refill line ever recorded. Nothin’s alive to t’row any of it. Da ledger resets to No Water Drawn, and 4.4 billion years after dat, on graph paper at four squares to da inch, somebody draws a pump." }
});
window.YOOPER.WATER_WAR_BANDS = {
  "Too Cool to Get Wet": "Too Cool to Get Wet, Eh",
  "Incidental": "Just Kinda Happened",
  "Backyard": "Out Back",
  "Neighborhood": "Down Da Block",
  "Municipal": "Whole Town",
  "Continental": "Da Whole Continent",
  "Fiction": "Holy Wah, Dat’s Made Up"
};
